\par\par \b \uc2 INDIAN MORNING BRIEFING: Sensex Gains Likely Capped At 14,800\b0\par\par\uc2 1,554 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juni 2009\par 05:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones Emerging Markets Report\par \uc2 EM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GLOBAL MARKETS \par DJIA         8497.18  -7.49   -0.09% \par Nasdaq       1808.06  +11.88  +0.66% \par S&P 500      910.71   -1.26   -0.14% \par FTSE 100     4278.46  -50.11  -1.16% \par Nikkei Stock 9663.48  -177.37 -1.80% \par Hang Seng    17842.83 -241.77 -1.34% \par Kospi        1393.42  +2.25   +0.16% \par SGX Nifty    4355.5   +15.5   +0.36% \par June contract \par  \par USD/JPY     95.70-74     -0.77% \par Range       96.77-95.53 \par EUR/USD     1.3943-48    +0.75% \par Range       1.3984-1.3809 \par  \par CBOT Wheat July     $5.660 per bushel \par Comex Gold          $936.00  +$3.80 \par Comex Gold Range    $928.40-$938.90 \par Nymex Crude (NY)    $71.03   +$0.56 \par  \par  \par CHARTING THE SENSEX \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bombay Stock Exchange's 30-stock benchmark index, the Sensitive Index, is likely to recover some lost ground Thursday, but charts indicate it will struggle to move past its major resistance of 14,800.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The index slipped below the lower end of the 14,550-15,600 range Wednesday - falling 2.9% to end at 14,522.84 - which is a major sign of weakness.\par \par \uc2 Considering the oversold condition in the hourly charts, there is a case for an intra-day rally up to 14,800.\par \par \uc2 The index is likely to drop to 14,300 once the anticipated short-term bounce is over. The Sensex has to close above 15,030 to indicate a reversal of the short-term downtrend.\par \par \uc2 As long as the index trades below 15,030, it will remain vulnerable to a drop to 13,500.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par US STOCKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Most U.S. stocks fell amid weakness in commodity and financial stocks, although strength in consumer and technology stocks helped the Nasdaq stay in the black.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The financial sector is dividing itself into strong and weak players. Even as some large banks repaid the government, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  cut ratings on 18 regional banks and pushed five into junk territory, saying their business looks less profitable going forward.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 JPMorgan Chase\f28 \uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2  all said they have bought back the preferred shares they issued to the government under last fall's bailout effort.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 JPMorgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  fell 2.3% to $32.73, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  dropped 3.1% to $139.73, and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2  slid 2.2% to $27.48.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ASIAN STOCKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian shares were mixed Thursday as investors tried to pin down a near-term direction for markets, with exporter shares in Tokyo hurt by ongoing resilience in the Japanese yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Nikkei was down and South Korea's Kospi Composite flat. China shares were boosted by the State Council's announcement it would continue to promote economic restructuring and consolidate a recovery, with the Shanghai Composite Index up.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index fell while Taiwan stocks were up. U.S. stock futures were a little higher in screen trade.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Trade was fairly quiet after a nondescript session on Wall Street. "There is not a lot going on, not a lot of interest and not a lot of factors influencing shares in either direction," said Barry Lindsay, director of research at First NZ Capital.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Markets were cautious about the economic outlook after some mixed data of late, and given worries about the financial sector. Even as some large U.S. banks repaid the government, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  cut ratings on 18 regional banks and pushed five into junk territory, saying their business looked less profitable going forward.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Exporter stocks were down in Japan with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mazda Motor\f28 \uc2 \f2  off 4.9% and TDK down 4.6%, though there was some buying in defensive drug makers and utility shares, with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Chugai Pharmaceutical\f28 \uc2 \f2  up 1.0%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Resource stocks were weak, with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nippon Steel\f28 \uc2 \f2  down 4.5%, and shipping shares failed to get a boost from a rise in the Baltic Dry Index, with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nippon Yusen\f28 \uc2 \f2  off 3.2%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But consumer electronics retailer Laox jumped 83% after a Nikkei report it was in talks with leading Chinese retail chain operator Suning Appliance on an equity deal that would make it the first big Japanese retailer to become a Chinese company's affiliate.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Property stocks were weak in Hong Kong, with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sino Land\f28 \uc2 \f2  down 2.4% and Henderson Land falling 2.4%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Singapore's Straits Times Index was choppy.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro was off its Asian highs. The U.S. dollar was at Y95.73, up from Y95.63 in New York but off an early peak of Y96.00, with the euro at $1.3951 from $1.3972 earlier, and 1.3945 in New York. The single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was at Y133.60, from a high of Y134.14.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par METALS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Spot gold edged up $5.50 to $940.90 a troy ounce from New York levels. LME three-month copper was up $25 from the kerb, at $4,980 a metric ton, though \f28 \uc2 \f2 JPMorgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  said the metal's correction was not over.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par OIL SUMMARY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Front-month Nymex crude oil futures were up 16 cents at $71.19 a barrel on Globex, after rising 56 cents in New York. Newedge Japan's Ken Hasegawa expected crude to hug a range of $69-$72 after a "neutral" stockpiles report from the U.S. Energy Information Administration Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CALENDAR OF EVENTS June 18 \par 0900 IST   Mumbai:     ICICI Bank CEO, Standard Chartered \par                        India CEO, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Union Bank of India\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par                        chairman, Citi India chief at \par                        banking summit \par 1030 IST   Bangalore:  Biocon chairman at event \par 1100 IST   Mumbai:     SEBI board meet \par 1200 IST   New Delhi:  Acer product launch \par 1200 IST   Mumbai:     \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nokia\f28 \uc2 \f2  press conference \par 1200 IST   New Delhi:  Inflation data for week ended June 6 \par 1230 IST   New Delhi:  HP product launch \par 1500 IST   Mumbai:     Piramal Healthcare shareholders meet \par Earnings:              Surya Roshni, TV Today Network, Opto \par                        Circuits \par 0700 GMT   EU:         ECB Governing Council & General \par 1330 GMT   US:         Tsy Secy Geithner testifies in \par                        Washington \par 1330 GMT   US:         IMF External Relations regular press \par                        briefing \par 1400 GMT   US:         June 6 DJ-BTMU Economic Barometer \par 1730 GMT   US:         Tsy Secy Geithner testifies in \par                        Washington \par 2030 GMT   US:         June 1 Foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Holdings \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES - Dow Jones/WSJ \par =WORLD BANK UPS CHINA 2009 GDP FORECAST; CAUTIOUS ON RECOVERY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The World Bank said Thursday it raised its forecast for China's economic growth this year to 7.2%, acknowledging the boost to the economy from Beijing's stimulus plan, though it cautioned that "it is too early to say a robust sustained recovery is on the way." (published 0300 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par =2ND UPDATE: GEITHNER,SUMMERS DEFEND PLAN TO BOOST FED POWERS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Obama administration officials Wednesday noted there's room for the president's regulatory reform proposal to evolve, but they also argued that there's no good alternative to making the Federal Reserve the uber-regulator of the U.S. financial system. (published 2201 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par =BUYERS STILL HEADING FOR GM CARS, TRUCKS DESPITE BANKRUPTCY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Car buyers appear undaunted by \f28 \uc2 \f2 General Motors Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 .'s bankruptcy, assuaging one of the auto maker's biggest fears heading into Chapter 11 protection. (published 2050 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par =2ND UPDATE:CHRYSLER TO RESTART PRODUCTION AT 7 ASSEMBLY PLANTS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chrysler Group LLC will restart production at more than half of its assembly plants which have sat idle since the company filed for bankruptcy six weeks ago. (published 2039 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par =FREDDIE MAC:$3B 5Y ISSUE FIRST WITHOUT CONCESSION SINCE CRISIS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Freddie Mac\f28 \uc2 \f2 's $3 billion five-year reference note could mark the return to the pre-crisis way of doing business for the government-owned mortgage giant. (published 2230 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par UPDATE: OIL FUTURES: NYMEX CRUDE UP FOLLOWING MIXED US OIL DATA \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Crude oil futures ended higher after dipping to a one-week low, as the latest U.S. petroleum data showed rising demand but also a continued abundance of supplies. (published 2021 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par WSJ(6/18) U.S. NATURAL-GAS SUPPLIES SURGE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The amount of natural gas available for production in the U.S. has soared 58% in the past four years, driven by a drilling boom and the discovery of huge new gas fields in Texas, Louisiana and Pennsylvania, a new study says. (published 2343 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par WSJ(6/18) US ELECTRICITY INDUSTRY TO SCAN GRID FOR SPIES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 WASHINGTON -- The electric-utility industry is planning a pilot initiative to see whether Chinese spies have infiltrated computer networks running the power grid, according to people familiar with the effort. (published 2329 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par NEC ELECTRONICS, TOSHIBA TO EXTEND CHIP TECH ACCORD WITH IBM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEC Electronics Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Toshiba Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . have agreed to extend an research and development accord with IBM Corp. allowing for the joint development of 28-nanometer semiconductor technology for use in consumer products, the three companies said Thursday. (published 0231 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES - Indian Media \par Citibank In Talks With India IT Cos On Tech Asset Sale - Economic Times \par India's \f28 \uc2 \f2 HDFC Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 , HDFC To Cut Deposit Rates - Economic Times \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Govt Could \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  In Reliance KG Gas Dispute - Economic Times \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Bathinda Oil Refinery IPO Eyed By Year-End - Economic Times \par BSNL Says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nokia\f28 \uc2 \f2 Siemens\f28 \uc2 \f2  Didn't Meet Tender Requirement - PTI \par India's Orissa Govt: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Posco\f28 \uc2 \f2  Proj Likely To Be Delayed - PTI \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fortis Healthcare\f28 \uc2 \f2  Frontrunner For Wockhardt Hospitals - Business Standard \par GMR Holdings Revokes 17M Pledged Shares - DNA Money \par Air India Workers,Mgmt Talks Fail To Get Breakthrough - PTI \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 18-06-09 0331GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 62500   \par \par \uc2 Dokument EM00000020090618e56i00004\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
