\par\par \b \uc2 India Rupee Falls To Over 1-Mo Low Despite RBI Action;Bonds Up\b0\par\par\uc2 472 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juni 2009\par 15:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                      As Of 1200 GMT \par                      Latest      Change \par    USD/INR           48.13       +0.38 \par    6.07% 2014 Bond   6.61%       -6 BPs \par    7.59% 2016 Bond   6.88%       -5 BPs \par    Call Rate         3.27%       -3 BPs \par    Forward Dollar Premium/Discount* \par    May              1.15       +0.015 \par    *Forward Dollar Premium/Discount is the midpoint of bid-offer spreads \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee fell to its lowest level in more than a month against the U.S. dollar Wednesday, hurt by a sharp slide in the local stock market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The dollar ended at INR48.13, up from Tuesday's close of INR47.75. It last closed higher May 15.\par \par \uc2 The rupee's fall came despite a moderate \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to support the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The dollar was strong on weak stocks and global cues, but the RBI stepped in and sold the dollar at INR47.95," said a dealer with a private bank.\par \par \uc2 Traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely sold between $200 million and $400 million.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's latest move was in contrast to its recent \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  when it was believed to have supported the dollar, which fell sharply following the reelection of the Congress party-led federal government last month.\par \par \uc2 Custodial banks sold dollars heavily. Dealers said these could be on behalf of foreign investors who were pulling out investments from local shares.\par \par \uc2 The Bombay Stock Exchange's Sensitive Index fell 2.9% to 14,522.84.\par \par \uc2 "We're looking at short-term strength in the dollar as the month-end and quarter-end demand (from crude oil importers) will come into play," said R.K. Gurumoorthy, head of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at \uc2 ING Vysya Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Also, advance tax payment for the first fiscal quarter indicates earnings at companies could be weak. As a result, stock markets may slip and "the dollar can target INR48.50 by end-June," Gurumoorthy said.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, government bonds ended up as the market took comfort from former RBI governor Bimal Jalan's view that there was room to lower interest rates further.\par \par \uc2 The 6.07% 2014 bond, the most traded paper in recent sessions, closed at INR97.75, up from INR97.50 Tuesday, while the 7.59% 2016 bond ended up at INR103.78 from its previous close of INR103.55.\par \par \uc2 "Interest rates should be lower in an economy which is reviving. If interest rates could be lower by 50 basis points, that would be good," Jalan told Dow Jones Newswires in an interview.\par \par \uc2 Dealers are now awaiting a bond buyback auction by the RBI Thursday for further cues.\par \par \uc2 -By Bijou George, Dow Jones Newswires +91 22 61456116; bijou.george@dowjones.com [ 17-06-09 1332GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1164 1847 62506   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020090617e56h000kw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
