\par\par \b \uc2 Rising India rupee hits resistance; more gains seen\b0\par\par\uc2 732 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juni 2009\par 11:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Up 10 pct from March record lows, stalling at 47/dlr\par \par \uc2 * Rising rupee extra burden for faltering export sector\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to check pace of gains rather than weaken it\par \par \uc2 By Swati Bhat\par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, June 9 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee has surged as cash floods into India as investors turn optimistic on the global outlook, but traders say authorities are trying to temper the rise so it does not disrupt an emerging economic pick-up.\par \par \uc2 The rupee is not at historic highs, indeed it is rising from a record low of 52.2 per dollar in early March, but its rapid 10 percent rise is causing difficulties for exporters just as the government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  are working to boost industry.\par \par \uc2 Traders say the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow its rise, half of which came in two days after the unexpectedly strong re-election of the Congress-led coalition last month, and they see 47 per dollar as its tolerance limit for now.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2  is stemming the fast pace of appreciation in the rupee as it hurts exports, which are crucial for manufacturing sector revival and therefore important for GDP sustainability," said Shubhada Rao, chief economist at Yes Bank.\par \par \uc2 Following the rupee's gains of as much as 4.5 percent in the two days after the election results, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped in to buy dollars -- just months after it had been selling them to support the rupee -- and traders estimate it has bought $4 billion to $5 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since then.\par \par \uc2 The rupee has made a couple of ventures through 47 per dollar since then, hitting a seven-month peak of 46.75 on June 3, at which point its was up 11.7 percent in three months, but has been unable to sustain the moves against the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "I suspect they are uncomfortable with the pace of appreciation that has come through, especially post-elections," said Robert Prior-Wandesforde, an economist with \uc2 HSBC\uc2  in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 "It's not a particular level, but just the magnitude of the recent move."\par \par \uc2 A Reuters poll after the election results found the rupee was expected to end 2009 at 46.25 per dollar. It was trading around 47.6 per dollar on Friday afternoon.\par \par \uc2 FOREIGN MONEY\par \par \uc2 Since mid-March, foreign funds have pumped in about $7.5 billion into a stock market that has rallied over 90 percent from its early March lows as investors regain their appetite for risk.\par \par \uc2 "We cannot yet say to what extent the current inflows are sustainable. If the RBI allows the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to appreciate very fast, tomorrow when funds dry up it will also depreciate equally fast, and they want to avoid this sharp variation," said Rupa Rege Nitsure, chief economist at \uc2 Bank of Baroda\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Foreign fund flows play a substantial role in driving the rupee. In 2007, record inflows of $17.4 billion helped the rupee gain more than 12 percent and the following year, net outflows of more than $13 billion saw it tumble by one-fifth.\par \par \uc2 Nitsure said falling exports and large trade deficits pointed at a small overvaluation of the rupee in the last month.\par \par \uc2 Exports have fallen in annual terms in the seven months to April, and the government expects them to keep falling until late 2009.\par \par \uc2 While not as export-driven as other Asian economies, exports account for about 15 percent of India's economy and are a key component of industrial output, which fell in three of the four months to March but beat expectations in April. [ID:nBOM501117]\par \par \uc2 Other trade-weighted measures suggest the rupee may still be a little undervalued despite its rapid rise against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  estimates the rupee's real effective exchange rate index has risen to 96.5 in May from 94.0 in April, below the fair-value level of 100.\par \par \uc2 However, the worries caused by the rupee's strength may ease if global optimism dims and risk appetite wanes.\par \par \uc2 Sean Callow, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Westpac\uc2 , expects equity markets to take a hit as the global recovery falters, especially in the United States, later this year.\par \par \uc2 "It will take a lot of pressure off rupee appreciation." (Editing by John Mair)\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INDIA\b0 \highlight\uc2 -RUPEE/\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2 |LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|SNI|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090615e56c000yu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
