\par\par \b \uc2 India Rupee Likely Soft In Forwards Mkt On Oil, Export Woes\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anant Vijay Kala   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 743 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Mai 2009\par 06:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee is likely to weaken in the forwards market as declining exports and rising crude-oil prices are expected to subdue the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent surge induced by the federal-election results, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 Refiners are expected to take cover on their likely higher import bill as crude has been moving up in the past few months. However, cover on the receivables for India's exporters may decline, leading to a demand-supply mismatch in the over-the-counter forwards market.\par \par \uc2 The dollar premium on the most-traded one-year contract could rise to INR1.25 in about a month and INR1.50 by July, said Moses Harding, head of global markets group at \uc2 IndusInd Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The one-year forward premium over the spot price closed at INR1.08 Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot and forwards markets to prevent a sharp rise in the rupee as a stronger local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could render \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's already dwindling exports less competitive. The market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could further boost premiums, they said.\par \par \uc2 "There is still demand for forward dollars and the rupee outlook seems bearish," \uc2 IndusInd Bank\uc2 's Harding said. "If the rupee actually goes for a toss, then there will be more demand for forward dollars."\par \par \uc2 One of the main threats to the rupee is from India's shrinking exports, which contracted 33% from a year earlier in April to record their seventh straight month of decline.\par \par \uc2 Trade deficit had narrowed from a year earlier in March - the latest month for which data is available - as import cost also fell because of lower oil prices.\par \par \uc2 But, that trend is expected to change with the recent gains in crude-oil prices. Crude oil accounts for about two-thirds of India's import bill.\par \par \uc2 The premium on rupee forward contracts had been locked in a narrow range over the past month, tracking the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot market and because of uncertainty over the composition of the new federal government.\par \par \uc2 But, a decisive mandate in favor of the Congress party-led alliance in the elections has bolstered hopes of bold economic reforms and improved foreign investor sentiment, sending the rupee more than 3% up Monday - its steepest intraday rise since 1998.\par \par \uc2 The rupee closed at INR47.49 to a dollar Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Ravindra Babu, a senior dealer at \uc2 Andhra Bank\uc2 , said a stable government and investments from abroad are the factors that could ease the pressure on the rupee.\par \par \uc2 But, dealers cautioned the sharp spike in the rupee this week was overdone with a high probability of a retreat to the near-INR50 levels it had been confined to in the recent past.\par \par \uc2 HSBC\uc2  is also bearish on the rupee and expects it to hit INR54 to a dollar by the end of 2009.\par \par \uc2 Royal Bank of Canada\uc2 , in a recent research note, said the rupee is expected to underperform most other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia's emerging markets, citing India's "horrible fiscal accounts and debt levels."\par \par \uc2 The government expects India's fiscal deficit at 5.5% of gross domestic product in the current fiscal year ending March 31, but analysts peg the gap at more than 10%, taking into account the deficits of state governments and off-budget items.\par \par \uc2 The threat of global rating agencies downgrading India to junk also looms large, which is a huge negative for the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Also, interest rates in India are unlikely to be further lowered soon, offering significant arbitrage opportunities over western markets which have near-zero interest rates. This could also boost forward premiums.\par \par \uc2 Generally, premiums are determined by interest-rate differentials between \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but the demand-supply situation also plays a role in countries such as India where the capital account is not fully convertible.\par \par \uc2 "Government bond yields are only going up and at this moment nobody is expecting a rate cut. So I don't think forward premium will come down drastically unless there is a substantial inflow and export booking," \uc2 Andhra Bank\uc2 's Babu said.\par \par \uc2 Premium on the one-year forward contract could rise to INR1.40 in the next couple of months and may go even higher if investment inflows aren't revived, Babu added.\par \par \uc2 -By Anant Vijay Kala; Dow Jones Newswires +91 22 61456114; anant.kala@dowjones.com [ 21-05-09 0400GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020090521e55l00036\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
