\par\par \b \uc2 RPT- \highlight22\uc2 India \highlight\uc2  cbank says sold $3.39 bln in March  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 274 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Mai 2009\par 04:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  (Repeats story issued late on Monday)\par \par \uc2  MUMBAI, May 11 (Reuters) - India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold a net $3.39 billion to check the rupee's losses in March, when the local unit touched a lifetime low, the bank's monthly bulletin showed on Monday.\par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank of India sold $3.75 billion, and bought $360 million in March. It had bought a net $230 million in February, after being a net seller of $29 million in January.\par \par \uc2  The partially convertible rupee <INR=IN> ended March 0.8 percent higher, after hitting an all-time low of 52.20 per dollar earlier in the month.\par \par \uc2  The rupee <INR=IN> has since recovered to close on Monday at 49.52/53, trimming its losses in 2009 to 1.7 percent mainly due to a recovery in local share prices since early March.\par \par \uc2  Foreign fund flows have a significant impact on the rupee's fortunes. Foreigners have bought a net of more than $1 billion in 2009, including about $900 million this month.\par \par \uc2  In 2008, when the rupee fell 19 percent, foreigners were net sellers of more than $13 billion of stocks. In 2007, the rupee rallied more than 12 percent as foreigners were net buyers of more than $17 billion of stocks.\par \par \uc2  India's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves <INFXR=ECI> fell to about $252 billion on May 1 from a peak of $316 billion last May, partly reflecting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the rupee.  (Reporting by Anurag Joshi; editing by David Stamp)     \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INDIA\b0 