\par\par \b \uc2 India cbank says sold $29 mln in January\b0\par\par\uc2 256 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2009\par 10:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, March 16 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold a net $29 million in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in January, sharply lower than $318 million in December, as the rupee stayed largely steady against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The Reserve Bank of India sold $1.084 billion in January and bought $1.055 billion, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monthly bulletin showed on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The partially convertible rupee <INR=IN> shed about 0.3 percent in January after having gained 2.9 percent in December. However, the rupee had lost 19.1 percent in calendar 2008 and is down about 5.4 percent so far this year.\par \par \uc2 Foreign funds pulled more than $13 billion from the domestic share market in 2008 in the wake of the global financial turmoil, and have withdrawn about $2.3 billion in 2009. The outflow has been one of the key factors hurting the rupee.\par \par \uc2 In 2007, record portfolio inflows of $17.4 billion had helped the rupee rise more than 12 percent.\par \par \uc2 India's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves have fallen sharply to $247.292 billion in mid-February from a peak of $316 billion last May, partly reflecting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 By 2:50 p.m. (0920 GMT), the rupee <INR=IN> was trading at 51.46/47 per dollar, marginally stronger than its previous close of 51.48/50. (Reporting by Swati Bhat; Editing by Ranjit Gangadharan)\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INDIA\b0 \highlight\uc2 -CBAN