\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 India \highlight\uc2  to use  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  bonds to fund deficit\b0\par\par\uc2 452 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Februar 2009\par 14:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Updates with details, background)\par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Feb 27 (Reuters) - India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Friday it will convert 450 billion rupees ($8.8 bln) of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds into federal bonds in instalments by the end of March, helping the government plug a budget funding hole.\par \par \uc2 The government had indicated last week it would seek the transfer of the funds to avoid overburdening the market with bond issues and causing a spike in yields before the fiscal year ends in March.\par \par \uc2 The government estimated this month its fiscal deficit would be 6.0 percent of gross domestic product in 2008/09, well above its intital estimate of 2.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 Accordingly, it sharply revised up its gross borrowing target for 2008/09 to 3.06 trillion rupees, more than double the initial estimate of 1.45 trillion rupees.\par \par \uc2 The extra borrowing will be made up of 1.16 trillion rupees of bonds and the remainder funded by the conversion of MSS bonds.\par \par \uc2 Concerns over swelling bond supplies have pushed up bond yields in recent weeks, with the benchmark 8.24 percent 2018 bond <IN082418G=CC> rising 109 basis points so far in 2009.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to step up its purchases of federal bonds in coming weeks to prevent a potential spike in volatility. [ID:nBOM115800]\par \par \uc2 Market Stabilisation Scheme (MSS) debt was introduced in 2004, mainly to absorb surplus cash generated due to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. For factbox, double click [ID:nBOM435887].\par \par \uc2 Since excess cash in the banking system would have fuelled inflation, the liquidity absorbed via MSS bonds were kept in a separate account. Although the government paid the interest on the bonds, it was not able to use the funds for expenditure.\par \par \uc2 "The situation has reversed in the recent period primarily on account of change in the external accounts resulting in attendant draining of primary liquidity reflecting the impact of Reserve Bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 "Consequently, the sterilised liquidity in the MSS cash account can be reversed by transferring the amount to the normal cash account of the government," it said.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  issued large amounts of MSS debt in 2007 and early 2008 as the rupee was rallying. But as foreign flows have turned, the rupee has slumped to record lows and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been buying back MSS bonds in recent months to support market liquidity. ($1=51.1 rupees) (Reporting by V. Ramakrishnan; Editing by John Mair)\par \par \uc2 INDIA-CBANK/MSS (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|SNI|M|D|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090227e52r00194\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
