\par\par \b \uc2 India Rupee, Bonds Fall; Trade Resumes After Mumbai Attacks\b0\par\par\uc2 655 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 November 2008\par 05:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--India's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was lower Friday while bonds slipped after the terrorist attacks on Mumbai, with expectations that risk-averse foreign investors would pull funds out, at least for the short term.\par \par \uc2 Trading resumed in financial markets after Thursday's halt, with attacks on hotels and other areas frequented by tourists and business travelers leaving 130 people dead; sporadic violence was continuing on Friday.\par \par \uc2 This "is probably the worst terrorist attack India has faced in its audacity, choice of locations, reported hostage taking and apparent targeting of Westerners," said analysts at the Eurasia Group.\par \par \uc2 "It seems likely that foreign investors for a long time will attach a higher political/security risk premium to their \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in India."\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar was recently around INR49.71, up from INR49.49 on Wednesday and expected by dealers to trade in a INR49.50-INR49.75 range; the rupee, which has fallen over 20% this year, has been one of the worst-performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia given foreigners have sold over $13.46 billion in India stocks.\par \par \uc2 "The bid/ask spreads are very wide and trading is quite thin as there is a lot of uncertainty in the market," said one \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market dealer.\par \par \uc2 Government bonds were lower despite a drop in weekly inflation and an assurance from rating agencies that the attacks were unlikely to have a material impact on the economy; the benchmark 8.24% bond was recently at INR107.60, from INR107.75 on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Dealers said the rupee's fall may be limited by potential \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Also, "foreign investors will return to \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  once the markets stabilize. That should happen very soon with easing inflation and interest rates steadily coming down," said R.K. Gurumoorthy, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at \uc2 ING Vysya Bank\uc2 , who expected the dollar to fall to INR46 by the end of March.\par \par \uc2 A dealer with a private bank added that markets had to some extent already carried a risk premium; "this is not the first time such an incident has happened. There may be some early panic in the market but it should be a short-lived setback and we could see the rupee stabilize very soon."\par \par \uc2 The main driver going forward for markets would be how much impact the attacks might have on the broader economy, given growth has already been showing signs of cooling, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 Growth slowed to 7.9% for the fiscal first quarter ended June 30 and is expected to slow further to 7.3% in the second quarter, according to a Dow Jones poll of 13 economists, with the data out at 0630 GMT.\par \par \uc2 Still, many economists doubted there would be a major, or prolonged, economic impact from the attacks.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were expected to open lower with the benchmark Sensex called down about 200 points, with declines likely in airline, hotel and other tourism-related shares; the expiry of monthly derivatives contracts Friday could exacerbate the early weakness.\par \par \uc2 Wednesday, the Sensex ended up 3.8% at 9,026.72.\par \par \uc2 Some however said the impact on stocks could also be short-lived: "It's hard to see how the tragic events will have anything but a negative impact on the market over the next five to 10 days. However, to the extent that a lurch down will make the market unquestionably cheap on valuation grounds, it could well lead to value investors looking afresh at this market in 2009," said Saurabh Mukherjea, head of equities at UK-based investment bank Noble.\par \par \uc2 Credit premiums were steady with the five-year credit default swap on \uc2 State Bank of India\uc2 , a proxy for India's sovereign credit, holding at 390-425 basis points after widening around 20 basis points Thursday.\par \par \uc2 -By Subhadip Sircar & Anant Vijay Kala; Dow Jones Newswires; 91 22 22884212; djn.in@dowjones.com [ 28-11-08 0417GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020081128e4bs0003e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
