\par\par \b \uc2 India Yld Curve May Steepen On Special Bonds Buyback By RBI\b0\par\par\uc2 By Subhadip Sircar & Anant Vijay Kala   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 812 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 November 2008\par 08:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's buyback program for special \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds is likely to spark demand for shorter-dated paper and lead to a steepening of the yield curve, traders say.\par \par \uc2 The first buyback of INR100 billion ($2 billion) of the so-called market stabilization bonds is slated Thursday, and banks which hold the bulk of such paper may offload them only at a hefty discount of 15-20 basis points to existing secondary market yields, they add.\par \par \uc2 "The RBI has clearly said that it will calibrate the buyback with the government bond auctions. So we can expect that the RBI will keep announcing buybacks, which should help boost demand for short-term paper. Naturally, the yield curve should also steepen," said Piyush Wadhwa, vice president of treasury at ICICI Securities Primary Dealership Ltd.\par \par \uc2 The spread between the one-year and 10-year paper, which currently stands at around 47 basis points, is likely to widen to 100 basis points in the next two to three months, said a bond dealing head with a foreign fund house.\par \par \uc2 On Saturday, the Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced a slew of measures to boost liquidity, including a 50-basis-point cut in the repurchase rate as well as a 100-basis-point cut in the cash reserve ratio as well as banks' statutory liquidity ratio.\par \par \uc2 It also said it will buy back market stabilization bonds issued earlier to provide more durable liquidity to the system.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  introduced the market stabilization scheme bonds in 2004 to soak up excess liquidity.\par \par \uc2 The RBI used the instrument extensively in 2007 to suck out excess rupee liquidity generated by its purchase of dollars in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to prevent a rapid rupee appreciation.\par \par \uc2 The RBI stopped selling these special bonds from early September as the rupee started losing steam and cash supply tightened.\par \par \uc2 As of Oct. 24, the outstanding amount of bonds under the market stabilization scheme stands at INR1.65 trillion.\par \par \uc2 The RBI will buy back INR50 billion of the 6.65% 2009 paper and another INR50 billion of 5.48% 2009 paper Thursday in its first buyback auction.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, the RBI will sell INR100 billion of bonds under its regular bond auction.\par \par \uc2 The Indian government is slated to sell INR390 billion of bonds in the fiscal second half as part of its normal borrowing program. Finance Minister P. Chidambaram recently said that the government will overshoot its borrowing program as it spends more on loan waivers to farmers and salaries to employees.\par \par \uc2 Over half of the fiscal second half borrowing is slated in the 10-year and above segment.\par \par \uc2 "Given the huge uncertainty all around, shorter-dated securities will be in fashion. Banks may not be willing to part with them," said a bond manager at the fund house.\par \par \uc2 RBI May Have To Pay A Hefty Yield Discount\par \par \uc2 Bond dealers say that the RBI will have to offer a hefty discount on the market yields to get a good response.\par \par \uc2 Most dealers say that the discount will be at least 15 basis points on the secondary market prices.\par \par \uc2 Currently, the 6.65% bond is yielding 7.13% and the 5.48% paper is yielding 7.19% in secondary market trade.\par \par \uc2 These paper have been illiquid for most part as banks hold them in their hold-to-maturity portfolio, which doesn't subject them to mark-to-market provisions. Most of these paper were issued when interest rates were low, but subsequently, interest rates hardened in India, eroding the value of the bonds.\par \par \uc2 Now, with the global financial crisis escalating, the RBI has started easing interest rates with the RBI's benchmark lending rate down by 150 basis points since the beginning of October.\par \par \uc2 "Banks may prefer to hold on to these paper as interest rates are headed south. The RBI will have to offer at least par rates (face value) for the bonds to make a success of the buyback," said Baljinder Singh, a dealer with \uc2 Andhra Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 However, some dealers say that the lowering of the statutory liquidity ratio by the RBI has led to INR400 billion of excess bonds in the system.\par \par \uc2 "There will be definitely some interest as banks have an excess of SLR bonds at the moment," said a bond dealer.\par \par \uc2 Under Indian rules, banks have to maintain a portion of their deposits in gold, cash or approved government securities, which ensures a captive demand for such bonds.\par \par \uc2 On Saturday, the RBI cut the statutory liquidity ratio by 100 basis points to 24% to boost liquidity in a cash-strapped interbank market.\par \par \uc2 -By Subhadip Sircar & Anant Vijay Kala; Dow Jones Newswires; 91 22 22884212; djn.in@dowjones.com [ 05-11-08 0740GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020081105e4b5000jw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
