\par\par \b \uc2 2ndUPDATE:India Stocks,Rupee Rally As Mkt Eyes More RBI Cuts\b0\par\par\uc2 1,133 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 November 2008\par 13:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones Asian Equities Report\par \uc2 AER\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Updates with closing stock, bond, rupee levels; adds prime minister's comments)\par \par \uc2 By Subhadip Sircar & Neelabh Chaturvedi\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--Indian stocks and the rupee rose Monday as investors welcomed the latest rate cut from the Reserve Bank of India and speculated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's focus on spurring economic growth means further easing is likely.\par \par \uc2 The RBI on Saturday cut its repurchase rate for the second time in as many weeks and injected more cash into the banking system to protect India's slowing economy from a worsening global financial crisis. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also for the first time put ensuring economic growth at the top of its policy agenda, a clear signal that it will chop interest rates further, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 "We maintain that the RBI is on the path of undertaking the mother-of-all-monetary-easings," said Rajeev Malik, economist at Macquarie Securities.\par \par \uc2 The RBI's move pulled down the interbank call rate to 7.25% from 21% Friday, and helped India assets continue to claw back some of their losses from a vicious selloff last month.\par \par \uc2 Stocks soared, with the 30-stock Sensex ending up 5.6% at 10,337.68, a near two-week high. The rupee climbed, pulling the U.S. dollar down to INR48.66 from INR49.45 at Friday's close.\par \par \uc2 Government bonds, however, fell, ignoring the heavy liquidity infusion as the RBI unexpectedly lowered a key reserve norm, the statutory liquidity ratio, which will cut the proportion of deposits banks must invest in sovereign debt. The 8.24% 2018 bond ended at INR104.54 as against INR104.97 at its close Friday.\par \par \uc2 Saturday, the RBI cut its repo rate by 50 basis points to 7.5%, adding to an earlier 100 basis point cut in October. It also lowered banks' cash reserve ratio further by another 100 basis points to 5.5%, which will add INR400 billion of liquidity.\par \par \uc2 Additionally, it cut banks' statutory liquidity ratio by 1 percentage point to 24%, a rate untouched for over a decade.The RBI will also provide refinance to banks for up to 1% of their deposits under a special liquidity adjustment facility valid for 90 days.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also allowed banks to borrow up to 1.5% of their deposits to fund non-banking financial companies.\par \par \uc2 More Cuts Eyed\par \par \uc2 Prime Minister Manmohan Singh, during a meeting with the heads of India's biggest companies Monday, said the government will protect growth.\par \par \uc2 "The government will take all necessary monetary and fiscal policy measures on the domestic front to protect our growth rates," Singh said.\par \par \uc2 " A crisis of this magnitude was bound to affect our economy and it has."\par \par \uc2 Economists predict additional cuts in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's key lending rate, or repurchase rate, as well as more cuts in the cash reserve ratio.\par \par \uc2 The slew of measures by RBI comes barely a week after it maintained status quo at its mid-term monetary policy review Oct 24.\par \par \uc2 Standard Chartered Bank expects the latest moves to infuse INR1.2 trillion of liquidity into \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's banking system.\par \par \uc2 However, heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to protect the rupee means that liquidity may still become tight going ahead.\par \par \uc2 India's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves fell $15.5 billion in the week ended Oct. 24, the most on record, to $258.4 billion.\par \par \uc2 "Given the pressure on the rupee due to the maturing of $52.7 billion of foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  loans by June 2009, and the need to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to prevent any free-fall in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , more liquidity-enhancing measures will be needed going forward, in our view," Standard Chartered said in a note.\par \par \uc2 The Indian rupee has lost nearly 20% against the US dollar so far in 2008 and is one of the worst performers.\par \par \uc2 Standard Chartered now expects the cash reserve ratio to fall to 4% by end March, while the repo rate may fall to 6% and the reverse repo rate to 5% by end June.\par \par \uc2 RBI Puts Growth Before Inflation Vigil\par \par \uc2 In its statement Saturday, the RBI said it cut the repo rate "in view of the ebbing of upside inflation risks as also to address concerns relating to the moderation in the growth momentum."\par \par \uc2 Analysts said that signaled the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  less concerned about inflation and is now focused squarely on spurring economic growth.\par \par \uc2 Tushar Poddar, economist at \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , said: "We think the downturn is spreading rapidly from the financial sector to real activity, and there are serious downside risks to our growth forecast of 7% in the next financial year."\par \par \uc2 India's economic growth fell to 7.9% in the fiscal first quarter ended June 30 as against 9% for the previous year.\par \par \uc2 Macquarie expects growth to slip to 7.2% in the current fiscal year.\par \par \uc2 The RBI revised its current year forecast to 7.5%-8% from 8% previously at its policy review.\par \par \uc2 Earlier Oct, RBI Governor Duvvuri Subbarao said that growth may moderate more in the next fiscal year if the financial crisis continues.\par \par \uc2 India's inflation rate has been rapidly easing as global commodity prices tumble on demand concerns as economies across the world slip.\par \par \uc2 Inflation rate fell for the fifth successive week to 10.68% due to a fall in the prices of manufactured goods, food and fuel in the week ended Oct. 18, from 11.07% a week earlier.\par \par \uc2 State-Run Banks Take Lead, Others Cautious\par \par \uc2 Some state-run banks have taken the lead and lowered interest rates after the RBI move.\par \par \uc2 Union Bank of India\uc2  Monday cut its prime lending rate by 50 basis points to 13.5%. \uc2 State Bank of India\uc2 , the country's biggest bank is yet to act.\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister P. Chidambaram said he will ask state-run banks to consider lowering interest rates.\par \par \uc2 "That (the RBI move) is the signal," Chidambaram told the Press Trust of India Saturday.\par \par \uc2 But the private sector players including ICICI Bank still want to wait and watch.\par \par \uc2 "The impact on deposit and lending rates will have to be watched over sometime," said Chanda Kochhar, joint managing director at ICICI Bank.\par \par \uc2 Analysts say a substantial fall in lending rates remain unlikely.\par \par \uc2 "In our view, bank lending rates are unlikely to fall substantially and immediately, as liquidity conditions remain tight and credit risk remains elevated," said Poddar.\par \par \uc2 "We expect banks to gradually lower first the deposit and then the lending rate by only 50-100 basis points, not least due to moral suasion and pressure from the RBI and government, particularly on public-sector banks," he said.\par \par \uc2 -By Subhadip Sircar & Neelabh Chaturvedi; Dow Jones Newswires; 91 22 22884212; djn.in@dowjones.com [ 03-11-08 1216GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument AER0000020081103e4b30000u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
