\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-India's markets tumble as investors run for cover\b0\par\par\uc2 By Saikat Chatterjee   \par \uc2 802 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Oktober 2008\par 13:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Updates market rates)\par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Oct 27 (Reuters) - Indian financial markets were pummelled on Monday as investors ran for cover in the face of the global financial crisis, knocking shares to their weakest level in three years and the rupee to a record low.\par \par \uc2 Money market rates rose sharply ahead of a holiday and as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to support the rupee.\par \par \uc2 Signs of a looming global recession and slowing corporate earnings heightened investor risk aversion across Asia, helping send India's benchmark index <.BSESN> down as much as 11.5 percent at one point and stoking worries that ebbing foreign capital would drag the rupee <INR=IN> down still further.\par \par \uc2 Shares in India's biggest bank, State Bank of India , plunged to a 1-\u189\'00\'BD year low on worries over bad debt provisions.\par \par \uc2 Its second-quarter net profit beat forecasts by rising 40.4 percent from a year earlier, but its shares ended down 8.6 percent after the bank ramped up its provisions for bad debts almost fourfold compared with the previous quarter. [ID:nBOM79617]\par \par \uc2 "It seems like a ball has fallen from the top of a tower and is on its way down and is gathering pace but has not yet reached the bottom," said Sanjeev Patkar, head of research at Dolat Capital Markets in Mumbai.\par \par \uc2 "How much of this is speculative and how much of this is forced liquidation remains to be seen but this selling is having a multiplier effect and we will see some more of this in the coming days before it recovers."\par \par \uc2 The 30-share index fell below 8,000 points to its lowest level since October 2005 before recovering some ground to end down 2.2 percent.\par \par \uc2 It has lost 58 percent so far this year, the worst performer in Asia in 2008 after China <.SSEC>, Hong Kong <.HSI> and Vietnam <.VNI>, as foreign investors have sold emerging market assets and repatriated their funds.\par \par \uc2 The index rose 47 percent in 2007 when foreigners pumped in a record $17.4 billion. Outflows so far this year are $12.6 billion.\par \par \uc2 RUPEE FORTUNES The partially convertible rupee <INR=IN>, whose fortunes are closely tied to foreign flows in and out of the stock markets, slid to a record low of 50.29 per dollar, even though traders reported \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop it up.\par \par \uc2 It ended trade a shade stronger at 49.87/88, but Kotak Mahindra Bank said it could fall to 52.5-54.00 per dollar by the end of the year as capital is drained from the country and pressure builds on the trade deficit from falling exports.\par \par \uc2 Interbank overnight lending rates jumped above 9 percent from 6.00/6.25 percent on Friday, well above the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's main short-term lending rate of 8 percent, but below 23 percent hit earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 Like many central banks around the world, the Reserve Bank of India has been pumping record amounts of liquidity into its money market to try to keep credit flowing.\par \par \uc2 It has slashed banks' reserve requirements to free up cash and a week ago knocked 1 percentage point off its key lending rate to try to bring stability back to \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's markets, strained by capital outflows, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and banks' reluctance to lend.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, it pumped 427.7 billion rupees ($8.6 billion) in via its money market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  to oil the wheels of the credit markets but rates remained elevated as banks borrowed heavily ahead of two market holidays this week.\par \par \uc2 "Due to the holidays banks are borrowing as much as they can now and also the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been selling dollars, which is squeezing rupee liquidity," a dealer with a foreign bank said.\par \par \uc2 Bond and money markets are closed on Tuesday for a local holiday. Stocks will trade briefly in the evening.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  opted not to follow last Monday's surprise rate cut with another one at a scheduled review on Friday, a decision which disappointed bonds and sent yields up.\par \par \uc2 But yields eased again on Monday as expectations rose that falling commodity and oil prices might persuade the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  it has room to ease policy again.\par \par \uc2 The 10-year benchmark bond yield <IN082418G=CC> fell 20 basis points to 7.60 percent.\par \par \uc2 "In the current global and local settings, the RBI should be putting more policy easing in place, because it has that flexibility and because the economy is screaming for it," Rajeev Malik, economist at Macquarie Securities, said in a note. (Editing by Ranjit Gangadharan)\par \par \uc2 FINANCIAL/INDIA (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|O|OIL|C|GRO|MTL|SOF|SNI|ABX|RNP|DNP|PCM|PEN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081027e4ar00157\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
