\par\par \b \uc2 Reverse repo to continue as policy tool-India cbank\b0\par\par\uc2 414 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Oktober 2008\par 12:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Oct 25 (Reuters) - India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to use its reverse repo rate as an active variable in its monetary policy and has not abandoned it, Governor Duvvuri Subbarao said on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 The reverse repo rate is the rate at which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  absorbs excess cash from banks and it has remained unchanged at 6.0 percent since July 2006, even though the repo rate, or key lending rate, has risen six times in the same period before being cut to 8.0 percent last Monday.\par \par \uc2 "We will continue to use that variable in our monetary policy. At the moment it is 6.0 percent and that's where we decided to leave it," Subbarao told news agencies a day after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's quarterly policy review.\par \par \uc2 "But it does not mean we have abandoned it. Far from it."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left all its key rates steady on Friday and said it had to strike an optimal balance between financial stability, price stability, anchoring inflation expectations and sustaining growth.\par \par \uc2 Subbarao also said the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) would continue with its established policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 "There are institutional policies of RBI, how we \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , how much we \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and in what markets we \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and we will continue that policy."\par \par \uc2 Heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rescued the Indian rupee from a record low of 50.15 per dollar <INR=IN> on Friday and kept the unit supported through the day even as the stock market plunged 11 percent to three-year lows.\par \par \uc2 Dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely sold between $3 billion and $4 billion via state-run banks to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and with investors pulling money out of emerging markets the outlook for the rupee was bearish.\par \par \uc2 Subbarao said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was monitoring the dollar liquidity situation, assessing the impact of recent measures such as easing oversees borrowing rules for corporates.\par \par \uc2 "For dollar liquidity we have said we are sensitive to maintaining the rupee and \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  liquidity. A number of measures have been taken," he said.\par \par \uc2 "We are monitoring the situation and if something more needs to be done we will do it but I am unable to say at this meeting what precisely we will do." (Reporting by Surojit Gupta and Charlotte Cooper; Editing by Anthony Barker)\par \par \uc2 FINANCIAL/INDIA-RATES|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|SNS|SNI|ABX|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081025e4ap0007h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
