\par\par \b \uc2 3rd UPDATE:Asian Shares Skid Into Weekend; Nikkei Sheds 9.6%\b0\par\par\uc2 1,140 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Oktober 2008\par 08:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds detail, quotes, closing levels; updates other information)\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Asian share markets crawled into the weekend on their hands and knees, with selling picking up late on Friday amid concerns the global economy was sliding towards recession as key companies issued cautious earnings outlooks.\par \par \uc2 "Absolute carnage in Asia as recession fears spread like a wild fire. Risk aversion is happening on a massive scale," was how Knight Equity Markets vice-president Ioan Smith described things.\par \par \uc2 Markets were looking increasingly disorderly with Korean shares slumping 10.6% by the close and Japan's Nikkei 225 ending down 9.6%; both indexes broke key levels to hit multi-year lows.\par \par \uc2 There was much caution before the weekend with U.S. stock futures down heavily in screen trade; S&P 500 futures were quoted 4.1% lower, Nasdaq 100 futures down 2.5% and Dow Jones Industrial Average futures down 301 points.\par \par \uc2 European spreadbetting firms were now calling stocks there well lower. "Volatility and uncertainty seem to be the watch words at the moment," said CMC Markets dealer Matt Buckland.\par \par \uc2 Key companies around Asia issued cautious earnings or earnings guidance: Japan's \uc2 Sony\uc2  and Korea's \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  were the most prominent of these.\par \par \uc2 That deepened concerns about the economic hit from the financial market crisis and spurred risk aversion, which showed up in a large rise in the Japanese yen.\par \par \uc2 "The theme of weaker global growth has replaced the issue of troubled banks for now and continues to weigh on investor psyches," said analysts at \uc2 UBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Analysts said emerging markets were feeling the contagion from developed markets, where the subprime crisis had begun. Indeed, recent data had shown a decline in emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  foreign reserves and fund outflows from several key markets.\par \par \uc2 In Korea the bourse wrapped up the week on a downbeat note, ending at 938.75, under 1000 for the first time since June 29, 2005; it fell 20.5% for the week and was down 35.2% now for October.\par \par \uc2 Sentiment was hurt by news gross domestic product growth slowed in the third quarter to its lowest quarterly level in four years; "we've just stepped into a dark tunnel," said \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  economist Oh Suktae.\par \par \uc2 Also, \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  fell 18.8% with its third quarter net profit of KRW1.219 trillion down from KRW2.191 trillion a year earlier and less than the KRW1.26 trillion forecast in a Dow Jones Newswires poll of analysts; \uc2 LG Electronics\uc2  shed 9.8% and \uc2 Hynix Semiconductor\uc2  fell its daily limit of 15%. \uc2 Kia Motors\uc2  dropped 14.8% after its weaker-than-expected earnings.\par \par \uc2 Japan's Nikkei 225 ended at its lowest level since April 2004, crimped by electronics shares as \uc2 Sony\uc2 's sharp outlook cut for earnings through March 2009 rippled through the sector; \uc2 Sony\uc2  shed 14% to a new year-low of Y1,972.\par \par \uc2 Australia's S&P/ASX 200 closed down 2.4% at its lowest level since November 2004 with financials tepid; Perpetual fell 6.8% and Axa Asia Pacific 8.0% after they suspended fund redemptions.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index was down 6.6% with \uc2 HSBC\uc2  shares falling 6.4% after \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  cut its target price on the stock.\par \par \uc2 Taiwan shares ended 3.2% lower with weakness in chip makers, while New Zealand's market shed 1% and Philippine shares 2.1%.\par \par \uc2 Singapore's Straits Times Index was down 7.3%; "we are still seeing fund redemptions. The ingredients are there for a rebound but there's just no catalyst," said a dealer at local house in Singapore. Indonesian shares were down 6.6% with Thai shares off 5.4% and India's Sensex down 7.7%.\par \par \uc2 The euro was the main decliner in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets with non-Japanese short-term speculators selling it against the yen on Japanese stock losses.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar dropped to Y94.93 from a high of Y97.98 with the euro at Y121.05 from Y127.04 earlier, falling to its lowest level since December 2002. The euro was down at $1.2740 from a high of $1.2975.\par \par \uc2 Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was under pressure, falling to US$0.6370, as was the British pound.\par \par \uc2 The recent rise in the yen was starting to cause some concern in Tokyo with finance minister Shoichi Nakagawa quoted by Kyodo as saying the government would closely watch the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stock markets and communicate with the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  and overseas authorities; still, few would take that as a sign that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a chance anytime soon.\par \par \uc2 The Korean won was on a roller-coaster ride with the U.S. dollar rising to KRW1,465 from KRW1,408.8 Thursday before suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell dollars took it back to KRW1,436.\par \par \uc2 Malaysia's ringgit hit a new low for 2008 against the U.S. dollar with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  spotted to sell the dollar in several places, including \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Indonesia.\par \par \uc2 "Dollar/Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets are trading with an almost universal bid and though some spot markets are being kept in check by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , discounts for Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  priced in the NDF market are deep and widening further," said \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  strategist Patrick Bennett.\par \par \uc2 Japanese government bonds were higher on safe-haven demand with lead December futures up 0.84 at 137.92; some said the swaps market was staring to price in the chance of a \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  interest rate cut.\par \par \uc2 Spot gold erased early gains with the U.S. dollar well bid and on the Nikkei's fall; the metal was at $712.50 an ounce, down $8.60 from New York.\par \par \uc2 Base metals were tepid with a trader at a global trading house in Shanghai saying buyers "were on strike; if copper prices are dropping 5% per day they are hardly going to buy. It will continue until prices stop falling and a rally looks like it could be sustainable." LME three-month copper was at $3,965 a ton, down $75 since Thursday's kerb.\par \par \uc2 Front-month Nymex crude gave up early gains despite expectations OPEC at its meeting later Friday would agree to cut output; it was down 27 cents at $67.57 per barrel.\par \par \uc2 -By Rosalind Mathieson and Kathryn O'Keeffe, Dow Jones Newswires; +65-6415-4140; Rosalind.Mathieson@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 24-10-08 0647GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020081024e4ao000an\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
