\par\par \b \uc2 India Rupee Dn To All-Time Low On Cues; Govt Bonds Surge\b0\par\par\uc2 626 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Oktober 2008\par 15:24\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                    As Of 1200 GMT \par                    Latest      Change \par  USD/INR           49.31      +0.31 \par  8.24% 2018 Bond   7.60%      -12 BPs \par  Call Rate         6.10%      +60 BPs \par  Forward Dollar Premium/Discount* \par  September         0.14       -0.06 \par  \par *Forward Dollar Premium/Discount is midpoint of bid-offer spreads. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee slumped to an all-time low against the U.S. dollar Wednesday as foreign investors continued to pull out of local stock markets caught in a downward spiral.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The dollar ended at INR49.31 Wednesday, up from INR49.00 in the earlier session. It hit an all-time high of INR49.50, breaching the previous INR49.30 high hit earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 The greenback also drew strength from its gains against global \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The dollar has been rising against global majors. If the trend continues then it wouldn't take much time for it to reach INR50.50, the next key psychological level," said L. Subramanian, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  head at ICICI Bank.\par \par \uc2 The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is also expected to depreciate in the next few sessions due to possible order cancellations by exporters.\par \par \uc2 "With the dollar showing no signs of correction, there is a possibility of export cancellations, which may lead to further depreciation of the rupee," he added.\par \par \uc2 Demand for the buck also got a boost as several participants who were absent from the market Tuesday, because of a strike by RBI workers, bought heavily to meet customer flows, said a senior dealer with a state-run bank.\par \par \uc2 The greenback later retreated on suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Reserve Bank Of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  that led to a wider selloff by market participants.\par \par \uc2 Subramanian estimated the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been selling an average of $500 million to $600 million each session as it seeks to slow the rupee's slide.\par \par \uc2 The Bombay Stock Exchange's Sensex closed down 4.8% at 10,169.90, dragged down by weaker global stock markets.\par \par \uc2 In the bond market, the 10-year benchmark bond surged for the fourth straight session as it resumed trading after the repo rate cut earlier this week. Trading in the 8.24% 2018 note had been halted for book closure ahead of coupon payments.\par \par \uc2 The note ended at INR104.25 Wednesday, up from INR103.47 last close. Yield on the paper fell 12 basis points to 7.60%.\par \par \uc2 "Liquidity has already eased significantly. If it remains at present levels for about a week then we could safely discount an extension in the current bonds rally," said a senior dealer with a state-run bank, who expects yield on the 10-year note to fall to 7.35% in about a week.\par \par \uc2 Bonds gave up some gains after Finance Minister P. Chidambaram said the government may overshoot its fiscal deficit target for the current fiscal year, cementing market fears that it will likely borrow more.\par \par \uc2 "The market had for a long time been anticipating this. But it seems to be the first time the government has admitted it," said a dealer with a private bank.\par \par \uc2 Later in the session, a senior finance ministry official said the government was likely to borrow INR500 billion more in the current fiscal.\par \par \uc2 Dealers said the market will now watch for the crucial weekly inflation data due Thursday for further cues.\par \par \uc2 India's wholesale price index-based inflation rate is expected to rise to 11.49% on year in the week ended Oct. 11, from 11.44% in the previous week, according to the median estimate in a Dow Jones Newswires poll of five economists.\par \par \uc2 -By Anant Vijay Kala; Dow Jones Newswires +91 22 22884212-18; anant.kala@dowjones.com [ 22-10-08 1324GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1164 1847 62506   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020081022e4am000qj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
