\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Asia: Indian Rupee To Weaken Despite Govt, RBI Moves\b0\par\par\uc2 By Harsh Joshi   \par \uc2 A Dow Jones Newswires Column   \par \uc2 859 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Oktober 2008\par 12:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee is likely to continue falling against the U.S. dollar in coming months, despite government and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  attempts to stem capital outflows from India's stuttering economy. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has lost nearly 10% of its value against the greenback since September, hitting a lifetime low of INR49.30 on Oct. 10, amid a broad scramble to buy dollars even as the pipeline of foreign money has sputtered.\f28 \par \par \uc2 India's authorities have responded with a slew of measures to bolster the domestic economy and attract capital - the latest being the Reserve Bank of India's surprise 100 basis point cut in its key lending rate Monday.\par \par \uc2 More steps are likely to follow, but as the likelihood of a global economic slowdown mounts and money is pulled from emerging markets, analysts say further countermeasures will at best only slow the rupee's decline. Some now predict a fall to INR52.00 to the dollar by the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 The rupee is likely to come under further pressure as India's balance of payments deteriorates.\par \par \uc2 "The options are clearly limited for both the government and the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 , beyond frequent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smoothen the volatility and allow a gradual depreciation," said Saugata Bhattacharya, vice president of business and economic research at \uc2 Axis Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Having relaxed the ceiling on overseas borrowings for local infrastructure companies and doubled the limit for foreign investment in domestic corporate debt to $6 billion, the government is considering a hike in the cap on investment in the insurance sector. It may also allow more foreign direct investment in cash-crunched sectors like retail.\par \par \uc2 Still, opening a whole number of windows to foreign direct investment is unlikely to change the picture over the next two to three months as capital remains scarce globally.\par \par \uc2 The world financial crisis has drastically cut investor appetite for emerging market assets. In sharp contrast to 2007, when foreign funds bought over $17 billion of Indian shares, they have pulled out over $11 billion so far this year.\par \par \uc2 The Bombay Stock Exchange's benchmark Sensex index has registered huge losses this year. It is currently just above the 10,000 point mark, having hit a lifetime high of 21206.77 points on Jan. 10.\par \par \uc2 "The rupee's recovery is intimately linked to global economic conditions improving and a return of risk appetite," said Vikas Agarwal, strategist at JP Morgan Bank, who pegs the dollar to cost INR49.00 by end of this year.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    The RBI May Have Run Out Of Tools \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The RBI seems unable to arrest the rupee's tailspin. Its 100-point cut, aimed at giving a leg-up to local stocks and thereby boosting investor confidence and easing the pressure on the rupee, produced only a modest bounce in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indeed, the rupee quickly retreated to near its intrasession lows and then breached the key psychological INR49.00 level late in the Indian day.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I think they (the RBI) are already doing the maximum. They can't do much more. It's highly unlikely the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will appreciate in the next three to six months," said Sailesh K. Jha, senior economist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays Capital\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He expects dollar/rupee to touch INR52.00 by the end of 2008.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The RBI has been \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market almost every day of late, by selling dollars from its reserves. It has started special \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for oil refining companies, which often dominate the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to procure dollars for their imports.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It has also twice raised interest rates on domestic accounts of Indians living abroad to bring in more dollar remittances.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But sustained \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , while damping sharp intraday fluctuations, is taking its toll on the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  data shows reserves to have fallen from over $300 billion on Aug. 1 to $274 billion in the week ended Oct. 10, a majority of that through dollar selling.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Balance Of Payments Deteriorating \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India, which imports nearly two-thirds of its total oil needs, is also feeling the strain in its trade account because of 2008's high oil prices. Oil imports doubled in the fiscal first quarter that ended June 30 versus a year earlier.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The current account deficit widened to $10.724 billion in the fiscal first quarter, compared with $6.301 billion a year earlier.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This almost eclipsed the surplus in India's capital account, and economists are projecting the country may fall into an overall deficit in its balance of payments over coming quarters.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 And, whatever relief will come from the sharp cooling in oil prices will be offset by a weaker rupee.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "With dwindling capital flows, it'll be difficult to counter a largely consistent current account deficit. This will naturally put further pressure on the rupee," said Bhattacharya of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Axis Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 , who tips the dollar to hover around INR50.00 by end of December.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Harsh Joshi, Dow Jones Newswires; 91 22 2288 4212; harsh.joshi@dowjones.com [ 20-10-08 1059GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4258 5095   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020081020e4ak000jm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
