\par\par \b \uc2 India Govt Bonds Rise On Hopes Of RBI Measures, Rupee Slips\b0\par\par\uc2 462 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Oktober 2008\par 15:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                    As Of 1200 GMT \par                    Latest      Change \par  USD/INR           48.53      +0.45 \par  8.24% 2018 Bond   7.91%      -3 BPs \par  Call Rate         10.25%     +150 BPs \par  Forward Dollar Premium/Discount* \par  September         0.19       +0.09 \par  \par *Forward Dollar Premium/Discount is midpoint of bid-offer spreads. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW DELHI (Dow Jones)--Indian government bond prices rose Wednesday on expectations of more measures from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to improve liquidity in the banking system.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The benchmark 8.24% 2018-dated bond ended at INR102.20, up from its previous close of INR101.95. Yield on the note fell by three basis points to 7.91%.\par \par \uc2 Cash in the banking system has dried up in recent weeks as companies made quarterly tax payments and the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets drained cash.\par \par \uc2 Banks remain reluctant to lend to each other, Finance Minister P. Chidambaram said, with call money rate spiking to 10.25% Wednesday from 8.75% on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 "The government and the Reserve Bank of India are agreed on the measures that have to be taken immediately," Chidambaram told reporters at a press briefing.\par \par \uc2 The comments bolstered market expectations of more steps by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to soothe shaky markets, after the RBI had last week aggressively slashed the proportion of deposits banks must set aside with it.\par \par \uc2 "Given that capital inflows have been scarce and it has a lot of headroom to cut the cash reserve ratio at a time when fears of sharp slowdown in growth are emerging, we could see some actions later today (Wednesday)," said Ramya Suryanarayanan, economist at DBS Bank.\par \par \uc2 However, gains were capped on speculation the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures may include a possible cut in the statutory liquidity ratio, a norm that governs the amount of sovereign debt banks must hold.\par \par \uc2 Under SLR, Indian banks need to maintain 25% of their deposits in gold, cash and government securities, hence any lowering of the requirement would hurt demand for government paper.\par \par \uc2 In the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the Indian rupee fell against the U.S. dollar as a slide in local shares raised concerns of more capital outflows from the country.\par \par \uc2 The dollar ended the day at INR48.53, up from its previous close of INR48.08.\par \par \uc2 "There was good buying interest on account of firm rates in the non-deliverable forwards market but \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  capped the gains," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at a state-run bank.\par \par \uc2 The Bombay Stock Exchange's 30-stock index down 674.28 points, or 5.9%, to end at 10,809.12.\par \par \uc2 -By Neelabh Chaturvedi; Dow Jones Newswires +91 11 43563311; neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com [ 15-10-08 1339GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1164 1847 62506   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020081015e4af000o3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
