\par\par \b \uc2 India Rupee Recovers Most Losses On Rate Cuts; Bonds Rise\b0\par\par\uc2 676 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Oktober 2008\par 15:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                    As Of 1200 GMT \par                    Latest      Change \par  USD/INR           48.01      +0.07 \par  8.24% 2018 Bond   7.98%      -13 BPs \par  Call Rate         10.50%     -25 BPs \par  Forward Dollar Premium/Discount* \par  September         -0.11      -0.01 \par  \par *Forward Dollar Premium/Discount is midpoint of bid-offer spreads. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee weakened against the U.S. dollar Wednesday to a more than six-year low on persistent worries about capital outflows, but recouped most of its losses after several central banks cut key policy rates, raising hopes of greater stability in global financial markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The dollar ended at INR48.01 Wednesday, up from INR47.94 in the previous session. It hit an intraday high of INR48.80, its highest level since July 2002.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. Federal Reserve cut its target federal funds rate 50 basis points to 1.5% Wednesday and was joined by the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2 , the Bank of England, the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Sweden's Riksbank and the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The (rate cut) news will help reverse the downtrend in global equity markets. Sentiment in India will also be lifted, which will be a positive for the rupee," said L. Subramaniam, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at ICICI Bank.\par \par \uc2 He said he expects sharp gains for the rupee from now on, driven by a likely recovery in local stock markets and expectations of heavy cancellations in the non-deliverable forward market Monday.\par \par \uc2 "I think the rupee should rise to the next technical level of INR46.58 on Monday," he said.\par \par \uc2 Indian financial markets will remain closed Thursday for a religious holiday.\par \par \uc2 Local dealers said the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't actively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market Wednesday as any move may not have stemmed the rupee's depreciation due to genuine outflows from local stocks amid the prevailing global economic uncertainties.\par \par \uc2 The RBI also refrained from heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the move could sap rupee liquidity from an already strained banking system, said a dealer with a foreign bank.\par \par \uc2 The Bombay Stock Exchange 30-stock Sensex index ended down 3.1% at 11,328.36, its lowest close in over two years. It had fallen as much as 8.2% to 10740.76 early in the previous session on foreign fund selling and a weak overnight close in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 In the bond market, government bond prices rose sharply after the global rate cut news raised hopes that the Reserve Bank of India may follow suit.\par \par \uc2 The 8.24% 2018-dated bond ended at INR101.70, up from INR100.84 in the earlier session. The yield on the benchmark note fell 13 basis points to 7.98%.\par \par \uc2 "Going by the reaction, it seems traders are discounting a 25-50-basis-point cut in the repo rate (RBI's key lending rate) after the news," said Baljinder Singh, bond dealer at \uc2 Andhra Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Singh notes, while a repo rate cut looks unlikely as inflation remains high, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could move to infuse liquidity by lowering banks' cash reserve ratio - the portion of deposits that banks need to set aside with the RBI in cash.\par \par \uc2 Bonds were also helped by a drop in crude oil prices, which fell to an intraday low of $86.05 a barrel. Crude oil later recovered to around $89/bbl.\par \par \uc2 However, initial gains were capped by a scheduled INR100 billion bond sale Friday that prompted traders to cut positions hoping for better prices to build stock at the auction.\par \par \uc2 Dealers will now scour the inflation data, industrial output data and bond auction results, all slated for Friday, for further cues.\par \par \uc2 The annual wholesale price index-based inflation rate is expected at 11.98% in the week ended Sept. 27, compared with 11.99% the previous week, according to the median estimate in a Dow Jones Newswires poll of six economists.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Anant Vijay Kala, Dow Jones Newswires; +91 22 22884212-18; anant.kala@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 08-10-08 1354GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1164 1847 62506   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020081008e4a8000zv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
