\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW:Dollar May Rise To INR50 By Jan On Global Woes-\uc2 UBS\b0\par\par\uc2 By Neelabh Chaturvedi   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 550 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Oktober 2008\par 10:46\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    NEW DELHI (Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee may plunge to lifetime lows against the dollar over the next three-four months due to a widening trade deficit and heightened risk aversion, the director of emerging markets \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy at UBS AG said Wednesday. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Reserve Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which is said to have been \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market sporadically, mightn't be able to stop the rupee's fall as it is legitimately preoccupied with forestalling a liquidity crisis, Nizam Idris told Dow Jones Newswires.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The dollar - now trading around INR48.66, a more-than-six-year high- may shoot up to INR50 by January, Idris said by phone and email from Singapore.\par \par \uc2 "With sentiment soured by extreme risk aversion and deleveraging, the dollar has remained well-supported against most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Idris said.\par \par \uc2 In the case of the rupee, the market is concerned by an adverse trade balance and the large amount of foreign investments already sunkin India, which are currently being pulled out, he said.\par \par \uc2 India's current account deficit widened to a record $10.72 billion in the fiscal first quarter ended June 30, compared with $6.30 billion a year earlier, the Reserve Bank of India said late last month. Idris expects the deficit to continue widening.\par \par \uc2 Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the Korean won to the Indonesian rupiah have been pummeled as the global financial crisis escalates, depriving these countries of the dollars they need to fund their ballooning current-account deficits.\par \par \uc2 The Indian rupee has lost 19% since January, marking a sharp reversal in fortunes for the unit, which was Asia's second-best performer last year with a 12% rise.\par \par \uc2 The dollar hit a lifetime high of INR49.07 against the rupee in June 2002.\par \par \uc2 Foreign investors have been net sellers of local shares worth $9.7 billion so far this year. The country's benchmark Sensex stock index has slid more than 46% since the beginning of 2008.\par \par \uc2 Idris said the current financial turmoil may make it difficult for the Reserve Bank to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively.\par \par \uc2 "Under such an environment, maintaining liquidity conditions precedes the need to control the exchange rates," he said. "Only when the need for liquidity eases - and this may take a while - would more aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  become easier."\par \par \uc2 Earlier this week, the RBI cut the amount of deposits banks have to set aside as cash with it for the first time in five years as overnight borrowing costs rose sharply.\par \par \uc2 For its part, India's capital markets regulator has said that it will remove a ban imposed last October on the issue of participatory notes with derivatives as underlying assets by foreign institutional investors. However, this move is unlikely to lead to heavy inflows anytime soon as global financial markets remain in a funk, Idris said.\par \par \uc2 To be sure, he expects the rupee to recover once risk appetite returns, as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would then focus on taming inflation by jacking up interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Idris expects inflation to stay above the RBI's comfort level of 7% by March 2009.\par \par \uc2 -By Neelabh Chaturvedi, Dow Jones Newswires; 91-11-4356-3311; neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com [ 08-10-08 0846GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020081008e4a8000mc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
