\par\par \b \uc2 India cash rate spikes after rupee hits 5-yr low\b0\par\par\uc2 By Saikat Chatterjee   \par \uc2 591 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Oktober 2008\par 10:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Oct 1 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee fell to five-year lows and overnight cash rates climbed to 18-month highs on Wednesday on fears foreign investors caught in a global credit crunch and turbulent markets would pull more money out of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The rupee slumped in early deals, and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop it up worsened a cash squeeze that has seen record amount of funds pumped into the banking system through the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's daily market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The cash rate surged to 17.50 percent, its highest since April 2, 2007, according to Reuters data, and nearly double the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's main lending rate of 9 percent, and a morning fund injection of more than 700 billion rupees ($15 billion) could only bring it down to about 15 percent.\par \par \uc2 "The deleveraging and the credit contraction that is happening in the world markets is having an impact here and that will continue to keep the rupee pressured downwards," said Mohan Shenoi, treasurer at Kotak Mahindra Bank in Mumbai.\par \par \uc2 The partially convertible rupee <INR=IN> fell as low as 47.2350 per dollar, its weakest since early June 2003, before it was propped up by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and strengthened back past 47 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 Bond yields fell as banks bought debt to meet their reserve requirements. The benchmark 10-year yield <IN082418G=CC> was at 8.49 percent below a previous close of 8.62 percent.\par \par \uc2 Analysts think money markets will remain tight for some time as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to keep propping up the rupee.\par \par \uc2 As well, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is selling 70 billion rupees of bills on Wednesday, and the government said last week it would auction 390 billion rupees of bonds by the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 "Everybody is rushing to cover their cash requirements, and this is also a holiday-shortened week and nobody wants to take any chances," said G. Bhaskar, a senior dealer with state-run Andhra Bank.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and debt markets were closed on Tuesday due to fiscal half-yearly closing of books by banks. Markets will shut again on Thursday for a national holiday.\par \par \uc2 And just as record buying of stocks by foreigners last year helped propel the rupee up more than 12 percent towards 10-year highs last year, the withdrawal of foreign funds this year has been a key factor in its fall of more than 16 percent.\par \par \uc2 Foreigners have sold a net $9.3 billion of Indian shares so far in 2008, including about $2 billion in September, after buying a record $17.4 billion last year. The stock market, which rose 47 percent in 2007, is down by more than a third this year.\par \par \uc2 There are other worries for the rupee, including widening trade and current account deficits.\par \par \uc2 "We may see the rupee test 48-49 per dollar in the next 3-6 months due to the deteriorating balance of payments conditions," Kotak Mahindra's Shenoi said.\par \par \uc2 The current account deficit <INCURA=ECI> widened sharply to $10.72 billion in the June quarter from $1.04 billion three months earlier as a sharp rise in oil prices widened the trade gap. [ID:nBOM201884].\par \par \uc2 Macquarie Securities expect the current account deficit to widen to $37 billion in 2008/09 from $17 billion in 2007/08. (Editing by John Mair)\par \par \uc2 INDIA-MARKETS/|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|SNI|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081001e4a1000hf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
