\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Asia loses ground on stock fall, credit woes\b0\par\par\uc2 684 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 September 2008\par 08:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Sept 18 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  drifted lower as growing fears of the financial turmoil sparked a global rout in stocks, with the South Korean won sliding more than 3 percent.\par \par \uc2 The won <KRW=> fell as far as 1,156.9 per dollar, nearing its four-year low at 1,165.8 hit on Tuesday, as foreign investors continued to dump local stocks amid a world-wide flight from riskier assets triggered by the U.S. financial system turmoil.\par \par \uc2 The Philippine peso <PHP=> fell as low as 47.29 to the dollar as investors dumped local stocks, but it was buffered by suspected dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is selling dollars to support the peso, from 47.28 to 47.29 (per dollar)," said a trader in Manila.\par \par \uc2 A second trader said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was trying to prevent the peso from falling below 47.30. Breaking that level would send the peso to its weakest level since May 2007.\par \par \uc2 The Taiwan dollar <TWD=TP> fell to a 7-1/2-month low at 32.284 per U.S. dollar, while the Singapore dollar lost half of a percent to 1.4365 against the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 The Indian rupee <INR=IN> lost almost one percent to 46.79 per dollar as falling local stocks ignited fears of capital outflows. But the rupee's loss was capped by suspected official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in a statement on Tuesday it would continue to sell dollars in the market either directly or through state-run banks to augment supply. [ID:nBOM324806]\par \par \uc2 The won has lost nearly 19 percent against the dollar so far this year, followed by a drop of almost 16 percent in the Indian rupee and a fall of 13 percent in the Philippine peso.\par \par \uc2 Global stock markets plunged after U.S. government's $85 billion bailout of insurer \uc2 American International Group\uc2  failed to calm investors, with the MSCI index of Asia stocks excluding Japan <.MIAPJ0000PUS> falling 3.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 Shares of \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  and larger rival \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  plunged on Wednesday, stoking talk that the surviving investment banks may have to join up with a commercial bank to survive the intensifying credit contraction.\par \par \uc2 Central banks around the world have pumped cash into money markets in response to a worsening credit squeeze as investors flee into safe havens of gold and government bonds.\par \par \uc2 The Thai baht <THB=TH> bucked by the regional weakness, rising 0.6 percent to 34.08 per dollar, as some investors cut their long dollar positions loaded up earlier this week following the failure of U.S. investment bank \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "We see unwinding Lehman exposure, but the market is thin," said a trader in Bangkok. \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Change on the day at   0618 GMT\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       104.45         104.13       -0.31\par  Sing dlr        1.4327         1.4290       -0.26\par  Taiwan dlr      32.261         32.082       -0.55\par  Korean won     1152.40        1116.00       -3.16\par  Baht             34.08          34.31       +0.67\par  Peso             47.27          47.08       -0.40\par  Rupiah         9410.00        9395.00  