\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-POLL-Indian rupee seen recovering to 45.50/dlr by end-Dec\b0\par\par\uc2 678 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 September 2008\par 04:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Repeats story issued late on Wednesday)\par \par \uc2 * Indian rupee seen ending 2008 at 45.50/dlr\par \par \uc2 * While stronger than now, will end 2008 down 13 pct\par \par \uc2 * Rupee seen strengthening to 45.00 by end-March 2009\par \par \uc2 By Swati Bhat\par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Sept 17 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee is expected to recover from recent two-year lows in coming months, aided by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but will still finish 2008 more than 13 percent below where it started, a Reuters poll showed on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The rupee hit 46.99 <INR=IN> per dollar on Tuesday, according to Reuters data, its weakest since July 2006 and just short of a five-year low. At the trough, it was down more than 16 percent in 2008.\par \par \uc2 The rupee recovered ground on Wednesday, closing at 46.33/35.\par \par \uc2 According to a median forecast in the Reuters poll of 10 economists, the rupee will climb back to 45.50 by the end of this year. That will still leave it 13.4 percent below its 2007 close of 39.41.\par \par \uc2 Economists said easing oil prices and a statement this week by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that it would continue propping the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  up, after much dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent weeks, were the two main factors likely to help it.\par \par \uc2 "There is some improvement over time in the current account deficit situation with the oil prices coming off so much, and hopefully if the global financial turmoil settles down, then we may see some capital inflows coming in as well," said A. Prasanna, economist with ICICI Securities.\par \par \uc2 After appreciating more than 12 percent against the dollar last year, the rupee has faced heavy pressure from rising risk aversion among foreign investors and record oil prices, as India imports about 70 percent of its oil.\par \par \uc2 Oil <CLc1> has fallen more than $50 per barrel from $147 in July, relieving pressure on the import bill, but market turmoil worldwide and concerns of slowing growth in India have seen foreigners sell a net $8.7 billion in shares so far this year.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) \uc2 said late on Tuesday it would continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  by selling dollars to support the rupee, either directly or through state-run banks. See [ID:nBOM324806].\par \par \uc2 It also took steps to attract more capital inflows by making interest rates more attractive on some foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and rupee deposits for non-residents.\par \par \uc2 But not everyone expected capital inflows to rise substantially by the end of this year.\par \par \uc2 Shubhada Rao, chief economist at Yes Bank, said the renewed financial turmoil stemming from the collapse of \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2  could dampen or delay inflows.\par \par \uc2 "We still have some pain left," Rao said.\par \par \uc2 A wildcard is whether authorities restart special market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  for refiners. Refiners are the largest dollar buyers in the local market and for about two months until July the RBI lent them dollars from reserves in exchange for oil bonds.\par \par \uc2 If this resumed, the rupee could rise by at least one rupee as a major source of dollar buying would be reduced.\par \par \uc2 "This facility might recommence if the RBI desires to send a signal that it is uncomfortable with the pace of INR weakness," Vikas Agarwal and Sid Mathur, economists at \uc2 JP Morgan Chase\uc2 , wrote in a note.\par \par \uc2 Following are forecasts for dollar/rupee by end-Dec and end-March 2009. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  ---------------------------------------------------------------\par  -  RESPONDENTS                        END-DEC 2008     END-MAR\par 2 009\par ----------------------------------------------------------------\par \par  J P MORGAN CHASE                      47.00             \par 46.25\par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 YES BANK\f28 \uc2 \f2                               47.00             No\par View\par  BANK OF MUSCAT                        46.10             \par 45.80\par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 ABN AMRO BANK\f28 \uc2 \f2                          45.50             \par 45.00\par  KOTAK MAHINDRA BANK                   45.50             \par 45.50\par  ICICI SECURITIES                      45.50             \par 45.00\par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 HDFC BANK\f28 \uc2 \f2                              45.50             \par 43.50\par  BANK OF BARODA                        45.00             \par 46.00\par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2                                   45.00             \par 44.50\par  STANDARD CHARTERED BANK               44.25             \par 43.50\par  ---------------------------------------------------------------\par  - MEDIAN                                45.50            \par 45.00\par  AVERAGE                               45.64             \par 45.01\par  ---------------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - (Additional reporting by V. Ramakrishnan and Anurag Joshi; Editing by Charlotte Cooper and John Mair)\f28 \par \par \uc2 INDIA-RUPEE/ (POLL, REPEAT)|LANGEN|Table|SNI   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080921e49i0001g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
