\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee rises on cbank steps, but stocks weigh\b0\par\par\uc2 361 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2008\par 09:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Rupee rises more than 1 pct after \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves\par \par \uc2 * Cbank sold dollars around 46.70, traders say (Updates to close)\par \par \uc2 By Swati Bhat\par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Sept 17 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee rose more than 1 percent in volatile trade on Wednesday, boosted by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's move to keep selling dollars and lift deposit rates for non-resident Indians, but stock market losses remained a weight.\par \par \uc2 The partially convertible rupee <INR=IN> ended at 46.33/35 per dollar, off a high of 46.25, and 1.2 percent stronger than 46.89/90 at the close on Tuesday. It fell to a low of 46.99 on Tuesday, its weakest since July 2006, according to Reuters data.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday the rupee fell as low as 46.75 due to heavy dollar demand from foreign institutional investors and oil companies, traders said.\par \par \uc2 They said the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's (RBI) commitment on Tuesday to continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the rupee helped it strengthen in early trade. [ID:nBOM320160]\par \par \uc2 "The rupee started trimming gains due to dollar demand from custodial banks, but then around 46.70 the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  appears to have stepped in which was followed by aggressive dollar selling by exporters and banks," a senior dealer with a private bank said.\par \par \uc2 Dealers said the rupee may rise towards 46 per dollar in the near term, helped by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's supportive stance.\par \par \uc2 India's main share index <.BSESN> fell 1.9 percent, taking its losses to more than 11 percent over the past seven days. [.BO] Foreign funds have so far sold a net $8.7 billion worth of stocks this year, helping push the rupee down nearly 15 percent.\par \par \uc2 Dealers said the dollar's strength versus other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  overseas was also weighing on the rupee.\par \par \uc2 In the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  futures market, the most heavily traded near-term contract closed at 46.54 per dollar. The total volume in the market was slightly higher than 52,000 contracts. (Editing by John Mair)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA-RUPEE (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|M|SNI|RBN|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080920e49h0004j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
