\par\par \b \uc2 Indian Rupee Falls To 23-Mo Low On Fund Outflows; Bonds Up\b0\par\par\uc2 644 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 September 2008\par 15:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                    As Of 1200 GMT \par                    Latest      Change \par  USD/INR           45.57      +0.46 \par  8.24% 2018 Bond   8.28%      -9 BPs \par  Call Rate         7.75%      -100 BPs \par  Forward Dollar Premium/Discount* \par  August            1.12       -0.10 \par  \par  \par *Forward Dollar Premium/Discount is midpoint of bid-offer spreads. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee fell to a 23-month low Thursday as concerns of fund outflows from local stock markets and the dollar's global strength continued to weigh on the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 The dollar ended at INR45.57, up from INR45.11 in the previous session. It touched an intraday high of INR45.57, last seen early in October 2006.\par \par \uc2 But dealers said the greenback was stonewalled by suspected massive \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Traders estimated the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could have sold close to $2 billion in the session as it sought to check the dollar's unrelenting rise.\par \par \uc2 But despite the cap, strong demand from foreign investors pulling out money from local shares and heavy oil bids lifted the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  close to its intraday high.\par \par \uc2 "The RBI was holding the dollar back. Had it not been in the market, the dollar would have shot through the roof," said a dealer with a private bank.\par \par \uc2 Traders said apart from the dollar's infused by the RBI there was no supply of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Even exporters are sitting on their cash piles betting on even better levels ahead to sell," said L. Subramanian, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at ICICI Bank.\par \par \uc2 Subramanian said the Non Deliverable Forward, or NDF, market had been tracking the surge in the dollar index, which measures the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's performance against global majors.\par \par \uc2 But the index seems to have reached its technical top and a correction could be round the corner.\par \par \uc2 "If the dollar reverses its gains overseas or if big flows hit the market, then we could see a major correction domestically," he said.\par \par \uc2 The Bombay Stock Exchange's 30-stock Sensitive Index, or Sensex, closed at the lowest in nearly two weeks, falling 338.32 points, or 2.3%, to 14,324.29.\par \par \uc2 In the bond market, government bond prices surged late in the session after the Chairman of the Indian Prime Minister's Economic Advisory Council said inflation looked to have peaked, leaving little room for further monetary tightening.\par \par \uc2 The 8.24% 2018-dated bond ended at INR99.75 Thursday, up from INR99.14 in the earlier session. The yield on the note fell nine basis points to 8.28%.\par \par \uc2 "My gut feeling is that inflation has peaked and will start tapering off. I do not basically see the case for further monetary tightening if inflation tapers off," Suresh Tendulkar told UTVi television news channel in an interview.\par \par \uc2 The market was also buoyed by speculation that the weekly inflation rate may ease to levels sharply lower than economists' expectations.\par \par \uc2 India's wholesale price index-based inflation rate was expected to come in at 12.01% on the year in the week ended Aug. 30, easing from 12.34% in the previous week, according to the median forecast of 12 economists in a Dow Jones poll.\par \par \uc2 However, data released after market hours belied all rumors, showing the inflation rate at 12.10%.\par \par \uc2 "The numbers seem very disappointing after the rumors that had been doing the rounds. We would have a bad session tomorrow with an opening at least 50-70 paise lower than today's close," said Ashish Vaidya, head of interest rate trading at \uc2 HDFC Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Vaidya said if stop-losses get triggered then it could mean a nose-dive for the benchmark 10-year government paper.\par \par \uc2 -By Anant Vijay Kala; Dow Jones Newswires +91 22 22884212-18; anant.kala@dowjones.com [ 11-09-08 1333GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1164 1847 62506   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020080911e49b000n0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
