\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee weakens past 45/dlr as shares weigh\b0\par\par\uc2 398 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 2008\par 05:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Losses in local share market weaken sentiment\par \par \uc2 * Foreign banks buy dollars\par \par \uc2 * Rupee seen falling to 45.50 in near-term (Updates to close)\par \par \uc2 By Swati Bhat\par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Sept 10 (Reuters) - India's rupee weakened past 45 per dollar for the first time since November 2006 on Wednesday as stock market losses raised expectations of more foreign fund outflows while strong dollar demand from foreign banks weighed.\par \par \uc2 The partially convertible rupee <INR=IN> ended at 45.12/13 per dollar, 0.62 percent weaker than Tuesday's close of 44.84/85.\par \par \uc2 It hit a low of 45.20 during the day's trade, its weakest since Nov. 16, 2006. The rupee at the close had lost 2.6 percent in the last six trading sessions.\par \par \uc2 "There was heavy dollar buying by some foreign banks today, which weakened the rupee," said Puneet Sharma, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at state-run Allahabad Bank.\par \par \uc2 "There is a possibility that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 45.05 and 45.15 levels, but the extent of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not much," he added.\par \par \uc2 The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy is not to take a view on the exchange rate but only to try and prevent volatility in the market in a flexible and liquid manner, its chief, Duvvuri Subbarao, said on Tuesday. See [ID:nBOM345682].\par \par \uc2 Dealers said losses in the local share market renewed worries of more foreign fund outflows, adding the rupee is likely to fall to 45.50 in the near-term in the absence of sizeable \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Indian shares <.BSESN> shed 1.6 percent to their lowest close this week, with banks slipping as global sentiment toward financials was dented by Lehman Brothers' woes. See [.BO].\par \par \uc2 The index is down 28 percent this year, with foreign funds having pulled out a net $7.4 billion, weakening a key support for the rupee.\par \par \uc2 In 2007, when foreign funds had bought a record $17.4 billion, the index rose more than 47 percent and the rupee gained 12.3 percent.\par \par \uc2 In the newly launched \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  futures market, the most heavily traded September contract ended at 45.2875 per dollar, with a modest volume of 41,328 contracts. (Editing by Mark Williams)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA-RUPEE (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|SNI|SNS|RBN|ABN|M|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080913e49a0001p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
