\par\par \b \uc2 Indian Rupee Up On Suspected RBI  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2 ; Govt Bonds Up\b0\par\par\uc2 460 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 August 2008\par 15:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                    As Of 1200 GMT \par                    Latest      Change \par  USD/INR           43.76      -0.09 \par  8.24% 2018 Bond   8.88%      Unchanged \par  Call Rate         8.00%      -100 BPs \par  Forward Dollar Premium/Discount* \par  August            1.12       +0.07 \par *Forward Dollar Premium/Discount is midpoint of bid-offer spreads \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee erased early losses to close higher against the U.S. dollar Wednesday supported by suspected aggressive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the Reserve Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for the second straight day.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The dollar ended at INR43.76, down from INR43.85 in the earlier session.\par \par \uc2 "I think two consecutive sessions of heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  should send a strong signal that the RBI doesn't like the dollar nearing the INR44.00 mark. The demand pressures, however, remain," said a dealer with a state-run bank.\par \par \uc2 Traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is suspected to have sold dollars from around INR43.90 level, dragging the pair down sharply.\par \par \uc2 Earlier, the dollar rose to as high as 43.92 helped by strong demand from oil companies to make month-end payments and the dollar's strength against major global \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It also drew strength from weaker local shares that raised concerns of fund outflows from local stock markets.\par \par \uc2 In the bond market, government bonds staged a turnaround to recover from early losses helped by improving liquidity in the banking system that enabled dealers to take fresh short-term positions.\par \par \uc2 The market was unfazed by a fresh rise in crude oil prices and the weekly inflation data due Thursday that some fear may climb to the psychologically important 13% level.\par \par \uc2 The 8.24% 2018-dated bond ended at INR95.90, up from INR95.85 in the previous session. Yield was unchanged at 8.88%.\par \par \uc2 A Dow Jones Newswires poll of eight economists showed a split in the middle with one half expecting inflation rate for the week ended August 16 to climb to 12.82% on year, while the other four forecasting 12.77%. It was at 12.63% in the previous week.\par \par \uc2 Dealers, however, cautioned there was pain ahead as liquidity was expected to deteriorate again.\par \par \uc2 "This (easing liquidity) was only a temporary relief as banks had overborrowed earlier. Bonds should see profit booking kicking-in tomorrow as liquidity would return to earlier tight levels ahead of the CRR (Cash Reserve Ratio) hike," said a bond dealer with a state-run bank.\par \par \uc2 The CRR will be hiked to 9% from the present 8.75% effective Aug. 30 and is expected to drain out about INR90 billion to INR100 billion from the system.\par \par \uc2 -By Anant Vijay Kala; Dow Jones Newswires +91 22 22884212-18; anant.kala@dowjones.com [ 27-08-08 1310GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1164 1847 62506   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020080827e48r000oj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
