\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Asia mostly down on Bernanke; c.banks  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kevin Yao   \par \uc2 793 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juni 2008\par 05:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, June 10 (Reuters) - Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell on Tuesday after Fed chief Ben Bernanke's warning on inflation fanned expectations of U.S. interest rate rises this year, but \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  lifted the won and the baht.\par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso <PHP=> briefly dipped to 44.45 per dollar, down 0.7 percent from Friday's close and its weakest level since October.\par \par \uc2  Philippine markets were closed on Monday for a holiday.\par \par \uc2  A trader attributed the peso's fall to a combination of domestic concerns about high oil prices and the dollar's broad rally, and suspected the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was again in the market to limit the peso's decline.\par \par \uc2  "I think the concerns locally are the primary driver of the weakening peso," he added.\par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar <SGD=> fell as far as 1.3695 to the U.S. dollar, down 0.6 percent from late Asian trade on Monday.\par \par \uc2  "It's due to Bernanke's statement," said a Singapore-based trader, who expected the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to move between 1.3645 and 1.3720 for the day.\par \par \uc2  The dollar jumped to a three-month high against the yen <JPY=> on Tuesday after Bernanke said on Monday that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would strongly resist an erosion in inflation expectations, convincing more investors that rate hikes could come this year.\par \par \uc2  Bernanke also highlighted inflation risks from surging energy prices and that the risk of a substantial downturn in the U.S. economy had receded, sparking broad dollar gains and sending U.S. bond yields soaring. [ID:nN09303720]\par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, U.S. Treasury Secretary Paulson declined on Monday to rule out \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market  to stabilise the dollar. [ID:nN09578167]\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, central banks in South Korea, \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Thailand \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the face of a broadly stronger dollar, traders said. [ID:nSP101255]\par \par \uc2  The South Korean won <KRW=> gained as much as 1 percent to 1,021.75 to the dollar, boosted by suspected dollar sales by the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities.\par \par \uc2  Despite suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the rupee, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  <INR=IN> eased a fifth of a percent to 42.932 per dollar.\par \par \uc2  The Thai baht <THB=TH>, which has fallen sharply in recent sessions, rebounded almost 0.9 percent to 32.93 per dollar, as the Bank of Thailand confirmed its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow the baht's fall.\par \par \uc2   "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  persists," said a Bangkok-based trader. "I think they want to repel speculators."\par \par \uc2  A second trader added: "If the baht breaks 33.50 level, it will move to 34.00. That means depreciation from 32.50 to 34.00 in 2 weeks, which is too fast. So they try to stabilise the baht."\par \par \uc2  The Malaysian ringgit <MYR=> fell a quarter of a percent to 3.272 per dollar.\par \par \uc2  The Chinese yuan <CNY=CFXS> hit a post-revaluation high at 6.9140 per dollar amid indications that Chinese policy makers still favour using a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fight inflation.\par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which maintains a tightening policy bias despite slowing economic growth, announced over the weekend that it would raise bank reserve requirements by 1 percentage point.\par \par \uc2  The yuan has gained 5.5 percent versus the dollar so far this year, the second best performer in Asia after the Taiwan dollar <TWD=TP>, which has risen 6.9 percent.  \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  Change on the day at 0751 GMT \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       106.67         105.09       -1.48\par  Sing dlr        1.3674         1.3612       -0.45\par  Taiwan dlr      30.352         30.299       -0.17\par  Korean won     1026.30        1032.10       +0.57\par  Baht             32.96          33.23       +0.82\par  Peso             44.40          44.14       -0.60\par  Rupiah         9327.00        9340.00       +0.14\par  Rupee            42.91          42.86       -0.13\par  Ringgit         3.2615         3.2630       +0.05\par  Yuan            6.9246         6.9230       -0.02\par  Change so far in 2008\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       106.67         111.33       +4.37\par  Sing dlr        1.3674         1.4382       +5.18\par  Taiwan dlr      30.352         32.443       +6.89\par  Korean won     1026.30         935.70       -8.83\par  Baht             32.96          33.68       +2.18\par  Peso             44.40          41.28       -7.03\par  Rupiah         9327.00        9390.00       +0.68\par  Rupee            42.91          39.41       -8.16\par  Ringgit         3.2615         3.3050       +1.33\par  Yuan            6