\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 1-Indian, Thai, Korean  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  fall as oil soars\b0\par\par\uc2 By Orathai Sriring   \par \uc2 617 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Juni 2008\par 12:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK, June 9 (Reuters) - The Indian and Thai central banks \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday to stem losses in the rupee <INR=> and baht <THB=> triggered by soaring world oil prices and more foreign selling in their respective stock markets.\par \par \uc2 The South Korean won <KRW=>, down 9.4 percent so far this year, also fell to a one-week low against the dollar although traders said they had not seen the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  selling dollars to curb the decline.\par \par \uc2 The won recovered some of its early losses as investors remained on the lookout for possible government dollar-selling, but is expected to remain under pressure from high oil prices and inflation that is likely to curb demand in Asia's fourth-largest economy.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Thailand blamed foreign speculators for pushing the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to its weakest level in five months, although it is still up 1.35 percent so far this year.\par \par \uc2 "We've seen some speculation and we are watching it," Assistant Governor Suchada Kirakul told reporters after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the second time in two days.\par \par \uc2 The baht closed at 33.23/25 after earlier hitting a five-month low of 33.41.\par \par \uc2 "The baht has weakened too fast. Our economic fundamentals have not changed overnight," she said.\par \par \uc2 "We will try to calm the baht's volatility. We don't want to see a move in one direction," she said, reiterating comments she made to Reuters on Friday. [ID:nBKK9980].\par \par \uc2 Suchada said the baht weakness was triggered by a sell-off in Thai shares last week, while importers of oil and other materials were still buying dollars on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Foreign investors sold a net 12 billion baht ($360 million) worth of Thai shares last week.\par \par \uc2 They have sold 27.4 billion baht worth of Thai stocks so far this year as inflation soared to near 10-year highs and anti-government protests renewed fears of political instability.\par \par \uc2 CURBING INFLATION\par \par \uc2 Asian central banks have traditionally \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation, which hurts exports, the main driver of growth for most of the region's economies.\par \par \uc2 But the focus is now shifting to containing inflation, with oil prices at record highs near $140 a barrel driving up costs and fuelling demands for higher wages.\par \par \uc2 The Indian rupee, which has dropped more than 8 percent since Jan. 1, fell as low as 42.93 to the dollar from Friday's close of 42.66/67 after a decline of more than 4 percent on the main stock index <.BESN>.\par \par \uc2 Foreigners dumped $879.1 million of Indian stocks in the four days to Thursday, bringing their net sales so far this year to $4.8 billion.\par \par \uc2 For a table on recent foreign buying and selling in Asian equities markets, double click on [ID:nSP71245]\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was seen selling dollars around 42.93 to the tune of $400-$500 million, three traders said.\par \par \uc2 "There had been heavy sporadic selling by state-run banks around 42.93 levels and the message seems to have been clear to the rupee shorts: 'Don't take it very far,'" one trader at a foreign bank said.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  usually \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  via a few state-run banks.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy is highly susceptible to soaring oil prices since it meets 70 percent of its crude oil needs through imports, which are paid for in dollars. When oil refiners buy dollars to meet their commitments, the rupee usually weakens. (Writing by Ed Cropley; Editing by Darren Schuettler and Jacqueline Wong)\par \par \uc2 ASIA-\b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  (WRAPUP 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|SNI|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080609e469000x8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
