\par\par \b \uc2 Indian  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  says bought $2.8 bln in March\b0\par\par\uc2 198 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Mai 2008\par 14:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, May 14 (Reuters) - India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought a net $2.8 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in March, taking its dollar purchases in the first three month of 2008 to $20.3 billion, rise, its monthly bulletin showed on Wednesday.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in February was $3.9 billion, and in January it was $13.6 billion.\par \par \uc2 The rupee <INR=IN>, which last November rose to 39.16 per dollar, its highest in nearly 10 years, fell 1.8 percent in the first three months of the year, ending March at 40.105/115.\par \par \uc2 After holding around 40 through much of April, the rupee's fall has accelerated sharply as record oil prices, a widening trade deficit, signs of slowing economic growth and worries of investment outflows have weighed.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit a 13-month low of 42.67, down more than 7 percent from the end of 2007.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought $74.9 billion in 2007 as the rupee rose more than 12 percent against the dollar. (Reporting by V.Ramakrishnan; Editing by John Mair)\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INDIA\b0 \highlight\uc2 -CBANK/\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2 |LANGEN|ABN|M|SNI|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080514e45e001gh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
