\par\par \b \uc2 MONEY TALKS: BRIC Tightening Shouldn't Harm Growth Outlooks\b0\par\par\uc2 980 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 April 2008\par 23:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Matthew Cowley \par    A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Four of the world's largest developing economies are going in the opposite direction to the Federal Reserve: they are restricting money supply just as the Fed has been opening the tap.\f28 \par \par \uc2 This shouldn't be seen as a contradiction; in fact, it is probably healthy for the global economy.\par \par \uc2 Inflation is on the rise around the world, driven primarily by higher food and energy prices. Authorities in Brazil, Russia, India and China - the so-called BRIC countries - are all tightening the supply of money in response to the threats.\par \par \uc2 That could stoke fears about weighing down further a global economy that is already spluttering from the developed-world financial crisis. According to the International Monetary Fund, the BRICs account for about 50% of global economic growth, so the prospect of slower growth in these countries could be significant.\par \par \uc2 The bigger threat, however, is that the BRIC economies have been growing faster than is probably healthy over the last few years. Export-driven growth has given way to strong domestic consumption, albeit still fueled by a commodities boom. That's more of a worry than any fallout from the global financial crisis, for now.\par \par \uc2 Economists remain sharply divided over the extent to which the economic slowdown in the U.S. and Europe will harm the developing world. One camp suggests there will be little or no effect, as emerging markets are now strong enough to stand on their own two feet. Another says there is far too much complacency about the risks.\par \par \uc2 For BRIC central bankers, the argument may be moot. They have to deal with what they can see, and that is inflation.\par \par \uc2 Stephen Gilmore, global emerging markets strategist at Banque AIG in London, referred to this as a "timing issue." These countries are "tightening because there are inflationary challenges" right now, he said. "If inflationary pressures were to soften, clearly the countries would have more potential" to ease monetary policy, he said.\par \par \uc2 Gilmore's own base case scenario is that "you get something of a slowdown but it's not too dramatic for the global economy."\par \par \uc2 For now, most short-term economic forecasts seem to have factored in much of the expected slowdown this year, as the inflation threat had been telegraphed well in advance. An average of the IMF's forecasts for the four BRIC countries indicates gross domestic product is likely to expand 7.2% this year, down from 8.5% in 2007, with a slowdown across all four economies.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    A Wide Range Of Policies \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 There is a broad difference among the four countries' growth rates, reflecting their different stages of development. Brazil, considered a more mature country, is at the low end, with forecast GDP growth of 4.8% for this year, according to the IMF. At the other end is China, which is expected to grow 9.3%. Russia and India are seen growing 6.8% and 7.9%, respectively.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Of the four, Brazil has the least to worry about inflation, thanks to its orthodox monetary policies and hawkish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Earlier this month, the bank raised rates by 50 basis points to 11.75%, the first hike in more than two years. Still, at 4.7%, inflation is above the government's year-end target of 4.5%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russia, India and China face more of a challenge. Russia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has raised rates but inflation is likely to come closer to 12% than the government's own forecast of between 9% and 10%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fearing social and economic consequences of continually raising interest rates, India and China have resorted to a broader set of tools to control inflation. Both have raised bank reserves and in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's case, levied new taxes, while China has \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  directly in lending markets and introduced price controls.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's main inflation gauge, the wholesale price index, was up 7.33% on year for the week ended April 12, well above the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's non-binding "comfort level" of around 5%. China's annual inflation reached 8.7% at the end of March, well above the target for the year of 4.8%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Brazil is helped by its floating exchange rate. A sharp influx of foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  - to buy its goods or seek out the higher interest rates - have driven the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to record strong points against the U.S. dollar. That in turn contributes to inflation-busting by making imports less expensive.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The other three get less help from their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Russia's and India's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are both managed through capital controls, while China's yuan is tightly pegged to a basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The yuan has been allowed to appreciate since 2005, but is still widely viewed as being undervalued.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Given the threat of inflation, slightly slower growth among the BRICs would be healthy for the global economy. Should inflation subside, or the developed-world slowdown become more infectious, then the BRIC governments seem to be well-placed to reverse their course of action and prevent any dramatic slowdown.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Matthew Cowley, a special writer with Dow Jones Newswires, writes a regular column about the fixed income market. He previously covered emerging markets from New York and before that the Brazilian economy and stock market for Dow Jones in Sao Paulo.)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Matthew Cowley, Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938 5692; matthew.cowley@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAmericas@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 29-04-08 2127GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1171 70779   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020080429e44t001d3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
