\par\par \b \uc2 Indian Rupee Gains After Ctrl Bk Aids Dollar Supply; Bonds Up   \b0\par\par\uc2 402 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Februar 2008\par 13:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                    As Of 1200 GMT \par                    Latest     Change \par  USD/INR           39.93      -0.29 \par  7.99% 2017 Bond   7.58%      -4 BPs \par  Call Rate         7.75%      -25 BPs \par Forward Dollar Premium/Discount* \par  January           0.30       +20 \par  *Forward Dollar Premium/Discount is midpoint of bid-offer \par  spreads. \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee pared some of the last two sessions' losses against the U.S. dollar Thursday as concerns over dollar shortage eased after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stepped in to supply greenbacks to the market, dealers said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 The dollar ended at INR39.93, down from its previous close of INR40.22 as possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  neutralized some of the cash crunch.   \par \par \uc2 "The concerns over the dollar shortage have eased slightly, but we have to wait and see if this is a long lasting effect. But I think the supply (of dollars) should be adequate for rest of the week," a private bank's dealer said.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar has scored high against the Indian unit recently as foreign capital inflows have trickled to a halt, creating a sudden scarcity of dollars and pushing the dollar-rupee to a five month high Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2 The shortage, combined with the recently flared up crude oil prices, has resulted in the last year's second best performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the region losing over 1.5% since the beginning of February.   \par \par \uc2 But some bankers are still positive on the rupee long term.   \par \par \uc2 "This is a temporary reflection on the fact that there are virtually no foreign institutional investment inflows. But once the sentiment improves, even a little bit of inflows can push the rupee back up," said P. Mukherjee, head of treasury at  \uc2 Axis Bank.\par \par \uc2 Mukherjee expects the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair to touch INR38.00 by the end of this year, if not sooner.   \par \par \uc2 In the government bond market, prices rose for the first time in four sessions as high yields attracted buyers.   \par \par \uc2 The benchmark 7.99% 2017 bond ended at INR102.69, up from INR102.43 last close. Its yield fell by 4 basis points to 7.58%.   \par \par \uc2 "These levels are a good time to enter the market. Also, cash liquidity showed some signs of improving," said a local fund manager.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Harsh Joshi, Dow Jones Newswires; +91-22-2288-4212; harsh.joshi@dowjones.com \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 21-02-08 1259GMT ]   \f28 \par \par \uc2 1164 1847 62506   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020080221e42l000ox\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
