\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee at 2-\u189\'00\'BD   month lows on dlr squeeze   \b0\par\par\uc2 360 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Februar 2008\par 04:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  (Updates to close)   \par \par \uc2  MUMBAI, Feb 13 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee fell to its lowest level in 2-\u189\'00\'BD   months on Wednesday as a dollar shortage in the market and concerns about foreign funds selling stocks prompted banks to sell the local unit.   \par \par \uc2  The partially convertible rupee <INR=IN> ended at 39.76/77 per dollar after falling as far as 39.8950 in afternoon trade, its lowest since November 27. The rupee had closed at 39.66/67 on Tuesday.   \par \par \uc2  "We've seen quite a lot of activity today, with supplies from the stock market reduced and importers buying dollars ... in addition to a lack of dollar supplies," said Rohan Lasrado, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at  \uc2 HDFC Bank.\par \par \uc2  Foreign funds bought more than $17 billion of stocks last year, a key driver of the rupee's rise of more than 12 percent in 2007. But the funds have sold more than $3.5 billion so far this year as weak global markets heighten risk aversion.   \par \par \uc2  That has led to a dollar shortage in the banking system, spurring dealers to buy dollars in the spot market and sell them in the forward market, pushing near-term \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  forwards into discount.   \par \par \uc2  Dollar/rupee forward premiums were quoting at a 0.03-3.20 percent discount for 1-5 months.   \par \par \uc2  "That is why exporters are postponing their dollar sales, as they traditionally book their receivables when it is trading at a premium," the head of trading at a foreign bank said.   \par \par \uc2  India's benchmark share index <.BSESN> rose 2.05 percent on Wednesday, snapping a five-day fall, but dealers said most of the gains were due to local fund buying rather than foreign buying.   \par \par \uc2  Data showed \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought $2.73 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in December, taking its dollar purchases in 2007 to $74.9 billion as it tried to stem the rupee's more than 12 percent rise in 2007.[ID:nBOM181447].   (Reporting by Catherine Bosley and Saikat Chatterjee; Editing by John Mair)     \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA-RUPEE (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|M|SNI|RBN|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080216e42d0002k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
