\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee slips as investors pare risky assets   \b0\par\par\uc2 328 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Dezember 2007\par 04:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2   (Updates to close)   \par \par \uc2  MUMBAI, Dec 17 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee on Monday slipped in tandem with local stocks, which had their largest fall in 4 months, as global investors pulled out of relatively risky emerging market assets on raised concerns of a U.S. slowdown.   The partially convertible rupee <INR=IN> ended at 39.54/55 per dollar, sharply down from the previous close of 39.34/35. It hit a near-decade high of 39.16 last month.   \par \par \uc2  "The rupee's fortunes are going to ride on equities, which are looking jittery at the moment, " said a senior dealer with a private bank.   \par \par \uc2  "If the stock market inflows stop, then the current level of outflows should drive the rupee towards 39.80 pretty soon," the dealer added.   \par \par \uc2  Emerging \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and equity markets fell as risk aversion grew and U.S. inflation data hit prospects for further rate cuts, raising concerns that the Federal Reserve might not be able to prevent the world's largest economy from slipping into recession.   \par \par \uc2 Strong U.S. inflation data gave the dollar a boost, helping it hit a fresh two-month high versus a basket of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in thin year-end trading.   \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there was strong demand for dollars in the offshore rupee market, indicating that carry trades, where investors sell low-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in favour of high-yielding assets like the rupee, were being reversed.   \par \par \uc2  Adding to the tentative mood were concerns India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would act decisively to stem the rapid appreciation in the rupee, which has gained about 12 percent this year.   \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought $64.5 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the first 10 months of the year.    \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ---------------------------------------------------------------\par                    MARKET SNAPSHOT\par  Bombay Sensitive Index         <.BSESN>\par  Indian rupee (/$)              <INR=IN>\par  ---------------------------------------------------------------\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  (Reporting by C.J. Kurrien, Editing by Mark Williams)\par \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA-RUPEE/(UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|Table|SNI|ABN|M|RBN|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020071220e3ch000gz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
