\par\par \b \uc2 Indian Corporate Debt-Few new issues ahead of outflows   \b0\par\par\uc2 400 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Dezember 2007\par 12:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Dec 10 (Reuters) - A few Indian companies are looking to raise funds ahead of heavy outflows from the banking system for tax payments by firms this week, which, traders said, could push up short-term rates. Traders estimate that around 300-350 billion rupees would flow out of the system for quarterly payment of taxes by firms.   \par \par \uc2 Cash supplies with banks has improved slightly due to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to check the rupee's rise.   \par \par \uc2 The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought about $52 billion through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the first nine months of the year, and is also seen having played an active role in the rupee market in October and November too.   \par \par \uc2 "A few borrowers are rushing issues before liquidity dries up. The other could wait for the next year when inflows start trickling in," the merchant banking head of a private bank, said.   \par \par \uc2 Traders said sentiment was also helped by expectations of a U.S. rate cut. The Federal Reserve is widely expected to lower the key federal funds rate by 25 basis points to 4.25 percent, which could push up flows into higher yielding assets of emerging economies like India.   \par \par \uc2 NEW ISSUES:   \par \par \uc2 India's state-run farm sector lender  \uc2 National Bank for Agriculture and Rural Development\uc2  (NABARD) is seeking to raise at least 2 billion rupees via a bond issue, which opens on Dec. 11, a banker associated with the issue, said.   \par \par \uc2 The three-year bonds will be sold in a book-building range of 9.05-9.50 percent and are rated "AAA" by CARE. The issue closes on Dec. 13. The arrangers are Yes Bank,  \uc2 HSBC\uc2 , ICICI Securities, Almondz,  \uc2 Kotak Mahindra Bank\uc2  and A.K. Capital.   \par \par \uc2 The yield on Reuters' benchmark five-year corporate paper <AAAIN10Y=> was at 9.22 percent on Monday, steady from a week ago. The spread between the 5-year corporate and government debt was at 125 basis points <0#AAAINBMK=>, up from 122 a week back.   \par \par \uc2 Corporate debt pages on Reuters: <INCORP02>-<INCORP-05>   \par \par \uc2 Spreads over federal bonds <0#AAAINBMK=>   \par \par \uc2 Commercial paper placements <IN/CREDENCE>   \par \par \uc2 Secondary market CP Prices <INCP01>   \par \par \uc2 Secondary market bond prices <0#INBOND=>   \par \par \uc2 Inter-corporate deposits <INCRED13>   \par \par \uc2 Recent ratings <IN/CREDENCE>   \par \par \uc2 Corporate bond yield curve <0#AAAINBMK=> <INCRED10> (Reporting by Anurag Joshi, editing by Prem Udayabhanu)   \par \par \uc2 INDIA-CORPDEBT/|LANGEN|SNI|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020071210e3ca000vw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
