\par\par \b \uc2 India Rupee Gains On Foreign Fund Inflows; Bonds Steady   \b0\par\par\uc2 470 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Dezember 2007\par 14:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    \par     As Of 1200 GMT \par                    Latest     Change \par  USD/INR           39.42      -0.07 \par  7.99% 2017 Bond   7.87%      Unchanged \par  Call Rate         7.85%      +15 BPs \par  Forward Dollar Premium/Discount* \par  November          0.42      +0.04 \par  *Forward Dollar Premium/Discount is midpoint of bid-offer spreads. .    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee rose to a two-week high against the dollar Tuesday as suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  prevented further gains, traders said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 The dollar ended at INR39.42, down from INR39.49 at Monday's close.   \par \par \uc2 According to Rohan Lasrado, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at  \uc2 HDFC Bank\uc2 , suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept the dollar/rupee tied to INR39.42 during the session.   \par \par \uc2 "Clearly, there are inflows with some foreign banks. The RBI came in at INR39.42 and took it (the dollar) to INR39.44. But it's not pushing it up," Lasrado said.   \par \par \uc2 With increasing expectations of a rate cut by the U.S. Federal Reserve next week, more foreign fund inflows are likely to find their way into India in the coming sessions, which may again fuel the rupee's ascent, analysts say.   \par \par \uc2 Strong foreign fund inflows into the country, especially into local stocks, have been a key factor helping the rupee gain over 12% against the dollar since January.   \par \par \uc2 The RBI has bought nearly $52 billion from the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in the year through September to stem the rapid rise of the rupee, which has hurt India's exporters.   \par \par \uc2 ICICI Bank said in a research note that the rupee will stay in a band of INR39.35-INR39.80 per dollar during this week "with a strong upside bias."   \par \par \uc2 However, one significant downside risk for the rupee comes from higher global crude oil prices. India imports over 70% of its crude oil requirements.   \par \par \uc2 In the debt market, the benchmark 10-year bond ended little changed Tuesday, while long-dated bond prices gained.   \par \par \uc2 The 7.99% 2017 bond ended at INR100.73, unchanged from its closing level Monday.   \par \par \uc2 Traders said the 8.35% 2022 bond and 8.20% 2022 bond gained on demand from one large insurance company.   \par \par \uc2 "There is talk that a large insurance company has moved out of the 7.99% 2017 paper and is buying into these bonds. Both these bonds are quite liquid now and are also seeing trader interest," said a dealer with a foreign bank.   \par \par \uc2 Traders said the 7.99% 2017 bond is likely to stay in a narrow range in the next session as the market expects more supply from an INR70 billion government bond auction scheduled for Dec.7-Dec.14.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Vipin V. Nair; Dow Jones Newswires; 91 22 2288 4212; vipin.nair@dowjones.com \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 04-12-07 1327GMT ]   \f28 \par \par \uc2 1164 1847 62506   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020071204e3c4000np\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfassung der Suche\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\